var e=JSON.parse(`[[101,"When the browser meets a \`<script>\` tag without the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes, it pauses the parsing of the HTML, loads the script file and executes it synchronously, and only then resumes parsing. This means the content after the script is not rendered until the script has finished its work, which can noticeably degrade the perceived loading speed.","When the browser loads an HTML page, it parses its content in sequence, building the DOM tree. On meeting an ordinary \`<script>\` tag without the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes, it stops the parsing process immediately. This is critical behaviour, because the script may contain commands that change the DOM (\`document.write\`, for example), and the browser has to ensure the tree is in a consistent state.\\n\\nThe sequence of events\\n\\n- The browser finds the tag \`<script src=\\"app.js\\">\`.\\n\\n- The parsing of the HTML is paused.\\n\\n- The browser starts loading the script file from the server (if \`src\` is given).\\n\\n- Once it has loaded fully, the script is executed synchronously.\\n\\n- Only after execution has finished does the parsing of the HTML resume.\\n\\nA practical example and the consequences\\n\\nConsider a simple HTML page:\\n\\nWhile the browser is loading and executing \`heavy-script.js\`, the user will see a blank screen. The page's content (\`<h1>\`, \`<p>\`) will not be painted until the script has finished. This leads to a poor user experience, especially on a slow connection.\\n\\nWhere this is applied and how to avoid the problems\\n\\nThis approach is necessary if it is critically important for the script to interact with DOM elements located immediately before it, or if the order in which several scripts run is strictly defined. In modern development, however, the following is recommended for optimizing performance:\\n\\n- Use the \`defer\` attribute for scripts that do not need to run immediately. They will be loaded in parallel with the parsing but will run only after the DOM has been built fully, before the \`DOMContentLoaded\` event.\\n\\n- Use the \`async\` attribute for independent scripts (analytics counters, for example), which can run at any moment after loading without blocking the parsing.\\n\\n- Place non-critical scripts before the closing \`</body>\` tag, so that the main content has time to be displayed.\\n\\nConclusion: ordinary script loading (without async/defer) blocks the parsing of the page, which can seriously degrade the perceived loading speed. Use it deliberately, only when a strict order of execution and immediate access to the DOM are required. In most cases \`defer\` is preferable for scripts that work with the DOM, and \`async\` for independent ones.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n    <title>An example</title>\\n    <script src=\\"heavy-script.js\\"><\/script>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n    <h1>An important heading</h1>\\n    <p>This text will appear with a delay.</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[102,"The DOM (Document Object Model) is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents that represents a document as a tree of objects. Every element, attribute and fragment of text becomes a node of that tree. The browser creates the DOM when a page loads, so that JavaScript can change the document's structure, style and content. Without the DOM, scripts would not be able to interact with a page's elements.","The DOM (Document Object Model) is not a programming language but a standard, cross-platform interface that lets programs and scripts access and update a document's content, structure and style dynamically. When the browser loads an HTML page, it parses it and builds a tree structure in memory out of the elements, attributes and text — the DOM tree.\\n\\nThe structure of the DOM tree\\n\\nThe tree consists of nodes. The main kinds of node are:\\n\\n- The document (Document): the root node, representing the whole document.\\n\\n- Element nodes: the nodes corresponding to HTML tags (<div>, <p>, for example).\\n\\n- Text nodes: they contain the textual content inside elements.\\n\\n- Attribute nodes: although they are part of the DOM specification, in most modern APIs they are not represented as separate nodes in the tree but are properties of element nodes.\\n\\nThe nodes are linked by parent-child-sibling relationships, forming a hierarchy.\\n\\nAn example of HTML and the corresponding DOM\\n\\nConsider some simple HTML:\\n\\nThe DOM tree for this document will look roughly like this (in simplified form):\\n\\n- Document\\n\\n- ├── Element: <html>\\n\\n- │ ├── Element: <head>\\n\\n- │ │ └── Element: <title>\\n\\n- │ │ └── Text node: \\"An example\\"\\n\\n- │ └── Element: <body>\\n\\n- │ ├── Element: <h1>\\n\\n- │ │ └── Text node: \\"A heading\\"\\n\\n- │ └── Element: <p>\\n\\n- │ ├── Text node: \\"A paragraph with \\"\\n\\n- │ ├── Element: <strong>\\n\\n- │ │ └── Text node: \\"bold\\"\\n\\n- │ └── Text node: \\" text.\\"\\n\\nHow is the DOM used?\\n\\nJavaScript reaches the DOM through the global \`document\` object. With the methods of the DOM API (\`getElementById\`, \`querySelector\`, \`createElement\`, for example) you can find nodes, create new ones, change their properties and add them to or remove them from the tree. Any change to the DOM tree is reflected on the displayed page immediately (this process is called reflow and repaint).\\n\\nA practical code example\\n\\nConclusion: understanding the DOM tree is critically important for any client-side development in JavaScript, since it is the main way of making a web page interactive and dynamic. The DOM serves as the bridge between static HTML and a living, script-driven interface.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n    <title>An example</title>\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n    <h1>A heading</h1>\\n    <p>A paragraph with <strong>bold</strong> text.</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\\n// We find the element by its id\\nconst header = document.getElementById('main-header');\\n// We change its text content\\nheader.textContent = 'A new heading';\\n\\n// We create a new element\\nconst newParagraph = document.createElement('p');\\nnewParagraph.textContent = 'This paragraph was added through JavaScript.';\\n\\n// We add it to the end of the document's body\\ndocument.body.appendChild(newParagraph);\\n\\n// We change the element's style\\nheader.style.color = 'blue';"],[103,"When you add an event listener, the handler function is registered for the given event on the element. When the event occurs, that function runs. Removing the event listener cancels the registration, and the function will no longer be called. It is important to remove listeners once they are no longer needed, especially on elements that are removed from the DOM, in order to avoid memory leaks.","Event listeners are functions that \\"listen\\" for particular events (a click, a mouse hover, keyboard input) on DOM elements and run when those events occur. The mechanism of adding and removing them is key to the interactivity of web pages and to managing resources.\\nAdding with addEventListener\\nThe \`addEventListener\` method registers the handler function you pass for an event on the target element. You can add several handlers for the same event on the same element. The handler receives an event object (Event) containing useful information.\\n\\nRemoving with removeEventListener\\nThe \`removeEventListener\` method removes a previously registered handler. For the removal to succeed you have to pass exactly the same arguments: the event type and a reference to the same function. Anonymous functions cannot be removed, so named functions are recommended.\\n\\nPractical use and important aspects\\n\\n- Managing memory: if an element is removed from the DOM but a listener on it remains, the function and the element may not be freed by the garbage collector (a memory leak). Always remove listeners before removing an element.\\n- The options parameter: in modern JavaScript \`addEventListener\` takes a third parameter — an options object with settings such as \`{ once: true }\` (run the handler only once) or \`{ passive: true }\` (to improve scrolling performance).\\n- The event phases: the third parameter may be a boolean \`useCapture\` value determining which phase (capture or bubble) the handler will be called in.\\nConclusion: use \`removeEventListener\` for cleanup when an event handler is no longer needed (when a component is destroyed in an SPA framework, for example), in order to make the application's behaviour predictable and avoid memory leaks.","const button = document.getElementById('myButton');\\nfunction handleClick(event) {\\n    console.log('The button was clicked!', event.target);\\n}\\n// We add the listener\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n\\n// We remove the listener\\nbutton.removeEventListener('click', handleClick);\\n// Now handleClick will no longer be called on a click."],[104,"To embed maps in web applications, three main libraries are used most often: Leaflet, OpenLayers and the Google Maps API. Leaflet is a lightweight, easy-to-use open-source library, ideal for basic maps. OpenLayers is a more powerful, comprehensive solution for complex GIS tasks, also open-source. The Google Maps API is a proprietary service from Google that provides rich functionality but requires licensing for commercial use.","In web development there are several key libraries for creating interactive maps, each with its own philosophy and area of application. The choice depends on the project's requirements: whether you need simplicity and speed, extended functionality for GIS, or integration with a popular service such as Google.\\nLeaflet\\nLeaflet is a lightweight (about 42 KB) open-source library that has become the standard for many projects thanks to its simplicity, good documentation and extensive community. It works excellently with tiled maps (OpenStreetMap, for example) and is easy to extend with plugins.\\n\\nOpenLayers\\nOpenLayers is a powerful open-source library oriented towards complex cartographic tasks and GIS (geographic information systems). It supports an enormous number of data formats (vector tiles, KML, GeoJSON) and provides fine control over the display.\\n\\nThe Google Maps API\\nThe Google Maps API is a proprietary service from Google that provides access to maps, streets, satellite imagery and a rich set of services (place search, routes). It requires an API key to be registered and, for intensive use, payment. Integration is simple but imposes licensing restrictions.\\n\\nConclusion: Leaflet is worth choosing for quickly introducing simple maps with open data. OpenLayers suits complex, customizable GIS applications. The Google Maps API is optimal when you need integration with Google's ecosystem (streets, business data) and a ready-made UI, and the project is prepared for the possible costs.","// An example of creating a simple map with Leaflet\\nconst map = L.map('map').setView([51.505, -0.09], 13);\\nL.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {\\n    attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'\\n}).addTo(map);\\n// Adding a marker\\nL.marker([51.5, -0.09]).addTo(map)\\n    .bindPopup('An example marker.')\\n    .openPopup();\\n\\n// An example of creating a map with OpenLayers\\nimport Map from 'ol/Map';\\nimport View from 'ol/View';\\nimport TileLayer from 'ol/layer/Tile';\\nimport OSM from 'ol/source/OSM';\\n\\nconst map = new Map({\\n    target: 'map',\\n    layers: [\\n        new TileLayer({\\n            source: new OSM()\\n        })\\n    ],\\n    view: new View({\\n        center: [0, 0],\\n        zoom: 2\\n    })\\n});\\n\\n// An example of initializing a Google Maps map\\nfunction initMap() {\\n    const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById(\\"map\\"), {\\n        center: { lat: 51.505, lng: -0.09 },\\n        zoom: 13,\\n    });\\n    new google.maps.Marker({\\n        position: { lat: 51.5, lng: -0.09 },\\n        map: map,\\n        title: \\"An example marker\\"\\n    });\\n}"],[105,"Canvas is a raster surface for drawing. The main limitation when working with a large number of objects is performance. Every object has to be redrawn every frame, which puts a heavy load on the CPU. There is no built-in system for culling invisible areas or optimizing repaints. For complex scenes it is better to use WebGL or SVG.","HTML5 Canvas provides a low-level API for drawing graphics on the fly with JavaScript. Being essentially a raster canvas, however, it has a number of fundamental limitations when you try to display hundreds or thousands of dynamic objects.\\nThe main performance limitations\\n\\n- No built-in DOM: unlike SVG, objects on a Canvas are not separate DOM elements. This means there is no automatic tracking of which object is where, and no events at the object level without additional logic.\\n- A full repaint: whenever the scene changes (objects move), you normally have to clear the whole canvas or part of it and redraw every object again. This can become a bottleneck.\\n- No hardware acceleration by default: standard 2D drawing (context2d) mostly uses the CPU. The canvas itself can be accelerated, but the drawing operations (fill, stroke) are not.\\nPractical problems and an example\\nImagine you are building a game with 10,000 particles. On every frame you have to update the position of every particle and draw it. The code might look like this:\\n\\nThis approach will quickly cause the frame rate to drop, because the browser performs thousands of drawing calls (arc, fill) on the CPU.\\nWays of optimizing it and the alternatives\\n\\n- Using WebGL: for complex 2D/3D graphics, WebGL (through libraries such as Pixi.js or Three.js) uses the GPU, which increases performance dramatically.\\n- Clipping: draw only the part of the scene that is visible in the viewport.\\n- Combining objects: if objects are static, draw them once on an off-screen canvas and then copy the finished image.\\n- Using sprite sheets to reduce the number of drawing calls.\\nConclusion: pure Canvas 2D is poorly suited to displaying thousands of interactive, dynamically changing objects. For such tasks WebGL or a hybrid approach with optimized drawing is worth considering. Canvas is ideal for drawing images, simple animation or processing video, where the whole scene is rarely redrawn.","// A simplified example of an animation loop with particles\\nconst particles = []; // An array of 10000 objects {x, y, vx, vy}\\n\\nfunction animate() {\\n    // 1. Clearing the whole canvas\\n    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);\\n\\n    // 2. Updating and drawing EVERY particle\\n    for (let p of particles) {\\n        p.x += p.vx;\\n        p.y += p.vy;\\n        ctx.beginPath();\\n        ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);\\n        ctx.fill();\\n    }\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n}"],[106,"For working with maps on the frontend, specialized JavaScript libraries are used most often. Leaflet is a lightweight, simple library for basic maps with tile support. Mapbox GL JS provides modern vector maps with high performance and customization. The Google Maps API is a popular commercial solution with extensive functionality, including search and routes. OpenLayers is a powerful library for complex GIS tasks with support for many data formats.","Integrating maps into web interfaces has become the standard for a great many applications, from delivery services to analytics dashboards. The main task is to display geographic data, provide interactivity (zoom, panning, markers) and often to overlay your own layers of information. Ready-made libraries are used for this, which abstract away the complexities of working with tile servers, projections and rendering performance.\\nPopular libraries\\n\\n- Leaflet: lightweight (about 42 KB) and easy to learn. Ideal for quickly introducing a basic map with markers, popups and vector layers (polygons, lines). It works with raster tiles from OpenStreetMap and other providers.\\n- Mapbox GL JS: a modern library for vector maps. The maps are drawn on the client side using WebGL, which lets you style every element (roads, buildings) dynamically and animate transitions smoothly. It requires an access token.\\n- The Google Maps JavaScript API: a commercial but very popular solution. It provides not only cartography but also ready-made services: Places (place search), Directions (route building) and Street View. Integration is simple but has limits and costs.\\n- OpenLayers: a powerful library for professional GIS applications. It supports an enormous number of data sources (WMS, GeoJSON, KML) and complex operations on maps. Its barrier to entry is higher.\\nA code example: a basic map with Leaflet\\n\\nThis code creates a map, adds a layer of OpenStreetMap tiles, a marker in the centre of Moscow and a simple polygon. Leaflet handles mouse and touch events for zooming and panning automatically.\\nCriteria for choosing an approach\\n\\n- Leaflet: choose it for simple projects when you need lightness, open data (OSM) and minimal dependencies.\\n- Mapbox GL JS: suits projects where custom map design, high performance with a large amount of dynamic data and a 3D view matter.\\n- The Google Maps API: optimal if you need maps that users will recognize instantly, and integration with other Google services (search, routes, reviews).\\n- OpenLayers: use it in complex analytical or enterprise systems where working with professional geodata and standards is necessary.\\nConclusion: the choice of approach depends on the project's requirements: for a quick prototype or a simple map with markers, Leaflet will do; for creating a unique, high-performance cartographic interface, Mapbox GL JS; for using maps that are familiar to users with built-in services, the Google Maps API; and for complex GIS tasks, OpenLayers.","// 1. Attach Leaflet's CSS and JS in your HTML\\n// 2. Initializing the map\\nconst map = L.map('map-container').setView([55.751244, 37.618423], 10); // Moscow, zoom 10\\n\\n// 3. Adding a tile layer (we use OpenStreetMap)\\nL.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {\\n    attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'\\n}).addTo(map);\\n\\n// 4. Adding a marker with a popup\\nconst marker = L.marker([55.751244, 37.618423]).addTo(map);\\nmarker.bindPopup(\\"Kremlin<br>Moscow\\").openPopup();\\n\\n// 5. Adding a simple polygon (a triangle)\\nconst polygon = L.polygon([\\n    [55.75, 37.60],\\n    [55.75, 37.65],\\n    [55.70, 37.62]\\n]).addTo(map);\\npolygon.bindPopup(\\"I am a polygon.\\");"],[107,"Figma is a cloud tool for interface design that lets designers create mockups, prototypes and design systems. Developers gain access to the mockups through shared links, where they can inspect elements: view styles (CSS properties), dimensions and spacing, and export assets (icons, images). To automate the process, plugins are often used that generate code or design-system tokens. This approach speeds up markup work and ensures the implementation matches the designer's intent.","Figma has become the standard tool for designing user interfaces thanks to its collaborative, cloud-based nature. In the development process it serves as the single source of truth for visual design, prototyping and creating design systems.\\nThe main stages of a developer's work with Figma\\n\\n- Access and familiarization: the designer grants access to the file through a link. The developer studies the structure of the mockups, the pages, the components and the state variants.\\n- Inspecting elements: in inspect mode (the right-hand panel) you can select any layer and see its exact CSS properties: dimensions, spacing, colours, shadows, font parameters. This spares you from \\"measuring\\" pixels by hand.\\n- Exporting assets: icons, illustrations and images can be exported in the formats (SVG, PNG, WebP) and resolutions you need, straight from the interface.\\n- Working with the design system: if components and styles have been set up in Figma, the developer can see every variant of buttons, input fields and other elements, and also the tokens (variables) for colours, typography and spacing.\\nIntegration into the development process\\nFor deeper integration, Figma's plugins and API are used. Plugins can generate React components, CSS-in-JS objects or even whole themes for an application from the styles in Figma, for example. This reduces routine work and lowers the number of errors when transferring the design into code.\\n\\nFigma also lets you leave comments right on the mockup, which makes discussing details between team members easier without having to switch to other messengers.\\nConclusion: working with Figma in interface development is a continuous process of inspecting, extracting data and communicating, which lets you translate a visual design into working code accurately and quickly, especially when there is a well-thought-out design system.","// An example: using colour tokens from Figma in CSS variables\\n:root {\\n  /* The colours taken from Figma's styles */\\n  --color-primary: #4361ee;\\n  --color-surface: #ffffff;\\n  --text-primary: #2b2d42;\\n}\\n\\n.button-primary {\\n  background-color: var(--color-primary);\\n  color: var(--color-surface);\\n  padding: 12px 24px;\\n  /* The padding values can match the spacing tokens from Figma */\\n}"],[108,"Enlarging the hit area of neighbouring buttons can make them overlap. A user pressing in the zone where they intersect may activate a button other than the one they wanted. This causes input errors and irritation and reduces the interface's accessibility, especially for people with motor impairments. It is important to design hit areas so that they do not intersect, providing clear boundaries between elements.","A hit area is the invisible zone around a visual element (a button, for example) that responds to a click or a touch. Enlarging it improves accessibility, especially on mobile devices or for users with limited motor control. When several buttons are placed close together, however, enlarging their hit areas without regard to the boundaries creates serious problems.\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Unintended activation: the user may press in the zone where the areas overlap, and whichever button handled the event first will fire (this often depends on the order in the DOM or on z-index). This leads to errors and confusion.\\n- Reduced usability: the interface becomes unpredictable, the user loses confidence in their actions, which increases the cognitive load and the time needed to complete a task.\\n- Accessibility problems: for people using assistive technologies or with a tremor, clear boundaries between interactive elements are critically important. Overlapping areas make navigation practically impossible.\\n- Difficulties in testing and maintenance: overlapping hit areas are hard to debug, since visually the buttons' boundaries may look correct while the logic of how they work is broken.\\nA code example and the solution\\nConsider an example of two neighbouring buttons with enlarged but overlapping hit areas.\\n\\nIn this example the \`::after\` pseudo-element extends each button's hit area by 10 pixels in every direction. If the buttons are placed right next to each other, those areas will intersect. The solution is to ensure a sufficient visual margin between the buttons that exceeds or equals the hit area's extension, or to design the hit areas so that they extend only in safe directions (for the left button, leftwards and up/down; for the right one, rightwards and up/down, for example).\\nConclusion: enlarging the hit area is necessary to improve accessibility and convenience, but it should be done deliberately, avoiding overlap with neighbouring interactive elements. The key principle is to preserve a minimum safe distance between hit areas, or to use a logical separation of zones.","<style>\\n  .btn {\\n    padding: 10px 20px;\\n    margin: 0; /* We removed the margins to make the problem obvious */\\n    position: relative;\\n  }\\n  /* We enlarge the hit area with a pseudo-element */\\n  .btn::after {\\n    content: '';\\n    position: absolute;\\n    top: -10px;\\n    bottom: -10px;\\n    left: -10px;\\n    right: -10px;\\n    /* background: rgba(255,0,0,0.2); */ /* For visualization */\\n  }\\n</style>\\n\\n<button class=\\"btn\\" id=\\"btn1\\">Save</button>\\n<button class=\\"btn\\" id=\\"btn2\\">Cancel</button>\\n\\n<script>\\n  // On a click in the overlap zone only one button will fire\\n  document.getElementById('btn1').addEventListener('click', () => {\\n    console.log('\\"Save\\" was clicked');\\n  });\\n  document.getElementById('btn2').addEventListener('click', () => {\\n    console.log('\\"Cancel\\" was clicked');\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[109,"Touch events in JavaScript are represented by the TouchEvent object. It contains lists of touches (touches, targetTouches, changedTouches) that let you track several fingers at once. Every Touch object inside a list contains the coordinates (clientX, clientY), the touch's identifier and the target element. These events are necessary for building interactive interfaces on mobile devices, such as zooming, dragging or swipes.","In web development for mobile devices and tablets, the standard mouse events (click, mousedown) are not well suited to handling multi-touch gestures. For that purpose JavaScript has a special kind of event — the TouchEvent. It is generated when a touch on a sensitive screen begins, moves and ends.\\n\\nThe contents of the TouchEvent object\\n\\nThe TouchEvent's key properties are three lists of \`Touch\` objects:\\n\\n- \`touches\`: the list of all the touches currently active on the screen.\\n\\n- \`targetTouches\`: the list of touches that began on the same target element as the current event.\\n\\n- \`changedTouches\`: the list of touches that changed and gave rise to this event (new fingers for \`touchstart\`, or removed ones for \`touchend\`, for example).\\n\\nThe Touch object\\n\\nEvery object in these lists represents a single touch by a finger and contains important data:\\n\\n- \`identifier\`: a unique numeric ID for tracking a particular finger throughout the gesture.\\n\\n- \`target\`: the DOM element the touch began on.\\n\\n- \`clientX\` / \`clientY\`: the touch's coordinates relative to the browser window (the viewport).\\n\\n- \`pageX\` / \`pageY\`: the coordinates relative to the whole document.\\n\\n- \`screenX\` / \`screenY\`: the coordinates relative to the device's screen.\\n\\n- \`radiusX\` / \`radiusY\`: the approximate dimensions of the touch area (for determining pressure, for example).\\n\\nThe event types and practical use\\n\\nThe main kinds of touch event:\\n\\n- \`touchstart\`: a finger has touched the screen.\\n\\n- \`touchmove\`: a finger is moving across the screen.\\n\\n- \`touchend\`: a finger has lifted off the screen.\\n\\n- \`touchcancel\`: the touch was interrupted by the system (an incoming call, for example).\\n\\nThese events are used to implement gestures: a swipe for navigation, pinch-to-zoom for scaling images, dragging interface elements.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nA simple example of tracking a single touch in order to drag an element:\\n\\nConclusion: the TouchEvent object and its contents are the foundation for creating responsive, intuitive touch interfaces in mobile web applications. It is worth applying whenever you need precise control over multi-touch interactions that cannot be implemented through mouse events or standard CSS properties such as \`touch-action\`.","let draggable = document.getElementById('box');\\nlet startX, startY, initialX, initialY;\\n\\n// Handling the start of the touch\\ndraggable.addEventListener('touchstart', function(event) {\\n  // We prevent the page from scrolling\\n  event.preventDefault();\\n  // We take the first touch from the list\\n  let touch = event.touches[0];\\n  // We record the starting coordinates of the touch and of the element\\n  startX = touch.clientX;\\n  startY = touch.clientY;\\n  initialX = draggable.offsetLeft;\\n  initialY = draggable.offsetTop;\\n});\\n\\n// Handling the movement of the finger\\ndraggable.addEventListener('touchmove', function(event) {\\n  event.preventDefault();\\n  let touch = event.touches[0];\\n  // We compute the offset\\n  let deltaX = touch.clientX - startX;\\n  let deltaY = touch.clientY - startY;\\n  // We apply the new position to the element\\n  draggable.style.left = (initialX + deltaX) + 'px';\\n  draggable.style.top = (initialY + deltaY) + 'px';\\n});"],[110,"On mobile devices the keyboard appears when an input field receives focus. This can be detected through a change in the dimensions of the visual viewport (the Visual Viewport API) or through the resize event on window. When the keyboard appears, the visual viewport shrinks in height. You can also listen for the focus and blur events on input elements in order to determine the moment indirectly.","Detecting the appearance of the on-screen keyboard is a key task for building convenient mobile interfaces. When the user taps an input field, the system shows the keyboard, which changes the area in which content is viewed. There is no direct \\"keyboardshow\\" event on the web, so developers use indirect methods.\\n\\nThe main approaches to detection\\n\\nThe most reliable modern way is to use the Visual Viewport API. The visual viewport is the part of the window that is currently displayed on the screen (excluding the areas covered by the keyboard).\\n\\nAlternative and traditional methods\\n\\n- The resize event on window: in many mobile browsers the appearance of the keyboard triggers a resize event on the main window. This behaviour is not standardized, however, and can lead to false positives.\\n\\n- Tracking focus: you can listen for the focus and blur events on input fields. The keyboard's appearance normally follows focus immediately. This gives no information about exact dimensions but does indicate the context.\\n\\n- Checking the active element: combining document.activeElement (which points to the input field) with a change in the window's dimensions gives a more accurate picture.\\n\\nThese techniques should be applied in order to scroll to the active field automatically, change the layout (hiding the bottom navigation bar, for example) or switch the input into full-screen mode.\\n\\nConclusion: use the Visual Viewport API for the most accurate and modern detection of changes to the viewing area caused by the keyboard, especially when knowing the remaining space precisely is critical for adapting the UI.","// An example using the Visual Viewport API\\nif (window.visualViewport) {\\n  window.visualViewport.addEventListener('resize', function(event) {\\n    const newHeight = event.target.height;\\n    const windowHeight = window.innerHeight;\\n    // If the visual viewport's height is considerably less than the window's height,\\n    // the keyboard is probably open\\n    if (newHeight < windowHeight * 0.7) {\\n      console.log('The keyboard has probably appeared');\\n      // Here you could, for example, scroll the input field into view\\n    } else {\\n      console.log('The keyboard is probably hidden');\\n    }\\n  });\\n}"],[111,"A synchronous request blocks the execution of the code until a response arrives from the server, freezing the user interface. An asynchronous request is sent and performed in the background, letting the main thread of code carry on working and handling other events. Once the server's response arrives, a special handler function (a callback) is called, which keeps the application responsive. Synchronous requests are considered obsolete and are hardly ever used in modern frontend work.","HTTP requests are the basis of a web application's interaction with the server. By the way they are performed they fall into synchronous and asynchronous, and this difference directly affects performance and the user experience.\\n\\nSynchronous requests\\n\\nA synchronous (or blocking) request stops the script's execution at the place where it was called. The browser \\"freezes\\" — the user cannot scroll the page, press buttons or interact with the interface until the server sends a response. This approach was widespread in the early days of the web, but nowadays it is considered an anti-pattern because of its catastrophic effect on UX.\\n\\nAsynchronous requests\\n\\nAn asynchronous (non-blocking) request is sent and control returns to the main thread of execution immediately. The browser carries on handling user events, animation and other code. When the server replies, a predefined handler function (a callback) fires, a Promise resolves, or the code after the \`await\` keyword runs. This is the basis of AJAX technology and of modern SPA frameworks.\\n\\nWhere they are applied\\n\\n- Asynchronous requests are used everywhere: loading data for a news feed, submitting forms without reloading the page, search autocompletion, infinite scroll.\\n\\n- Synchronous requests have practically been banished from frontend development. In rare cases you may meet them in utility scripts or specific server environments where blocking is acceptable.\\n\\nConclusion: always use asynchronous HTTP requests in client-side JavaScript. It is a mandatory condition for building smooth, responsive web applications that do not make the user wait. The synchronous approach is an archaic method that leads to a \\"hanging\\" interface.","// An example of a synchronous request (DO NOT USE THIS IN PRACTICE)\\nconst request = new XMLHttpRequest();\\nrequest.open('GET', '/api/data', false); // The third parameter 'false' makes the request synchronous\\nrequest.send();\\n// The code here will not run until the response arrives\\nconsole.log(request.responseText);\\n\\n// An example of an asynchronous request using the Fetch API and async/await\\nasync function fetchData() {\\n  try {\\n    // The code is not blocked while the request is in flight\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    console.log('The data has arrived:', data);\\n    // We update the interface with the new data\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Request error:', error);\\n  }\\n}\\n// We call the function. The interface stays responsive.\\nfetchData();\\nconsole.log('This line will run straight away, without waiting for the response from the server.');"],[112,"The preload and prefetch attributes are hints to the browser telling it how to load resources in advance. Preload tells the browser to load a critically important resource (a font or a script, for example) immediately, because it will be needed on the current page. Prefetch suggests that the browser load a resource for future navigation (for the next page, for example) in the background, when there is spare network bandwidth. This lets you speed up the display of the current page and make transitions between pages faster.","The \`preload\` and \`prefetch\` attributes are mechanisms provided by modern browsers for optimizing the loading of resources. They let a developer tell the browser explicitly which files should be loaded in advance, before they are needed, which can improve a site's perceived performance considerably.\\n\\nPreload for critical resources\\n\\nThe \`preload\` attribute is used for resources that are critically important to the current page and will be used in the near future. The browser loads such a resource with a high priority without blocking the page's painting. This is especially useful for fonts, scripts or styles that are discovered late (through JavaScript, for example).\\n\\nIn this example we preload a font, stating the resource's type through the \`as\` attribute, which helps the browser set the priorities correctly and apply its security policies (CORS, for example).\\n\\nPrefetch for future navigation\\n\\nThe \`prefetch\` attribute is intended for resources that will be needed on later pages the user may navigate to. The browser loads such resources with a low priority when the network is idle, so as not to interfere with the loading of the current page.\\n\\nThis approach is often used in single-page applications (SPAs) to preload the code of sections the user may reach.\\n\\nPractical use and the differences\\n\\n- Preload is applied to the current page: for fonts, critical CSS/JS, images above the fold.\\n\\n- Prefetch is applied to future pages or actions: for loading the code of the next route in an SPA, or gallery images that may be needed.\\n\\n- The key difference is in the priority: \`preload\` is high, \`prefetch\` is low.\\n\\nConclusion: use \`preload\` to speed up the loading of critical resources on the current page, and \`prefetch\` to improve the speed of navigation between pages, especially in SPAs. These are simple but powerful tools for optimizing performance.","<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"critical-font.woff2\\" as=\\"font\\" type=\\"font/woff2\\" crossorigin>\\n\\n<link rel=\\"prefetch\\" href=\\"next-page-bundle.js\\" as=\\"script\\">"],[113,"Validation checks whether data matches the rules you have set (the format of an email address, for example). Sanitization cleans or transforms data to remove dangerous elements (HTML tags, for example). Validation answers the question \\"is the data correct?\\", while sanitization answers \\"is the data safe?\\".","The main concepts\\nValidation and sanitization are two key processes in handling data, especially when working with user input. Validation checks whether the data meets particular criteria (the length of a string, the format of an email address, a range of numbers). Sanitization, by contrast, is aimed at removing or transforming potentially dangerous parts of the data, such as HTML tags or SQL injections.\\nExamples and the differences\\nImagine a registration form with a field for a user name. Validation might check that the name contains only letters and digits and that its length is between 3 and 20 characters. Sanitization will remove any HTML tags the user may have entered, in order to prevent XSS attacks.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Web forms (registration, comments)\\n- API endpoints (checking the input parameters)\\n- Databases (protection against SQL injection)\\nValidation is normally performed first, in order to filter out data that is obviously incorrect. Sanitization is applied afterwards, in order to make the data safe before it is stored or displayed.\\nConclusion: validation and sanitization are complementary practices. Validation guarantees that the data has the expected format, while sanitization protects the system from malicious content. Use them together for reliable handling of user input.","// An example of validation in JavaScript\\nfunction validateUsername(name) {\\n  const regex = /^[a-zA-Z0-9]{3,20}$/;\\n  return regex.test(name);\\n}\\n\\n// An example of sanitization (removing HTML tags)\\nfunction sanitizeInput(input) {\\n  return input.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '');\\n}"],[114,"Parsing a URL means taking an address apart into its constituent pieces: the protocol, the domain, the port, the path, the parameters and the anchor. It is needed to extract data from the address bar, for navigation and for handling requests. In JavaScript, for example, the URL object lets you get at these components easily.","What is URL parsing?\\nParsing a URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the process of taking an address string apart into its logical components, such as the protocol, the host, the port, the path, the query parameters and the fragment. It is a fundamental operation in web development, since browsers and servers use URLs to identify resources and pass data.\\nThe main components of a URL\\n\\n- The protocol (scheme) — http, https or ftp, for example.\\n- The host — a domain name or an IP address.\\n- The port — the port number (80 by default for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS).\\n- The path — the path to the resource on the server.\\n- The query parameters (the query string) — a set of key=value pairs, beginning with ?.\\n- The fragment — the part after #, used for anchors on the page.\\nAn example of parsing in JavaScript\\n\\nWhy is URL parsing needed?\\nURL parsing is used to:\\n\\n- Extract data from the address bar (search parameters, for example).\\n- Validate and normalize a URL before sending a request.\\n- Navigate in single-page applications (SPAs) with React Router or Vue Router.\\n- Create links with dynamic parameters.\\nConclusion: URL parsing is a basic technique necessary for working with web addresses. It is applied in client-side and server-side applications for handling requests, routing and passing data. Understanding a URL's structure helps you avoid errors when working with HTTP and improves an application's security.","const url = new URL('https://example.com:8080/path/page?name=John&age=30#section');\\nconsole.log(url.protocol); // 'https:'\\nconsole.log(url.hostname); // 'example.com'\\nconsole.log(url.port); // '8080'\\nconsole.log(url.pathname); // '/path/page'\\nconsole.log(url.search); // '?name=John&age=30'\\nconsole.log(url.hash); // '#section'\\n// Getting the parameters\\nconsole.log(url.searchParams.get('name')); // 'John'"],[115,"XSS (cross-site scripting) is an attack in which an attacker injects a malicious script into a web page. The main means of protection are: escaping data on output, validating and sanitizing input, using a Content Security Policy (CSP), and setting the HttpOnly flag on cookies. It is also important to avoid dangerous functions such as innerHTML and to use safe template engines.","What XSS is and why it matters\\nXSS (Cross-Site Scripting) is a kind of attack in which an attacker injects malicious JavaScript code into a web page, which is then executed in the victim's browser. This can lead to cookies being stolen, redirection to phishing sites or the page's content being changed. Protection against XSS is critical for any web application that works with user data.\\nThe main methods of protection\\n\\n- Output escaping: converting special characters (\`<\`, \`>\`, \`&\`, for example) into their HTML entities before they are inserted into the page. This prevents the data from being interpreted as code.\\n- Validating and sanitizing input: checking and cleaning the data from the user on the server. Removing \`<script>\` tags, for example, or using a whitelist of allowed tags.\\n- Content Security Policy (CSP): an HTTP header that restricts the sources of scripts, styles and other resources. \`Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self'\`, for example, permits only scripts from the same domain to run.\\n- The HttpOnly flag on cookies: setting the \`HttpOnly\` flag when creating a cookie prevents access to it through JavaScript, which protects session data from being stolen.\\n- Avoiding dangerous functions: do not use \`innerHTML\`, \`document.write()\` or \`eval()\` with user data. Use \`textContent\` or safe template engines instead (React escapes its output automatically, for example).\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nProtection against XSS requires a comprehensive approach: escaping output, validating input, using CSP and safe APIs. These methods matter especially when developing applications with user-generated content (forms, comments, chats). Applying these practices reduces the risk of attacks and increases users' trust.","// Dangerous code (vulnerable to XSS)\\nconst userInput = \\"<script>alert('XSS')<\/script>\\";\\ndocument.getElementById('output').innerHTML = userInput;\\n\\n// Safe code (escaping)\\nconst safeOutput = userInput.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;');\\ndocument.getElementById('output').textContent = safeOutput;\\n\\n// An example of CSP in HTML\\n// <meta http-equiv=\\"Content-Security-Policy\\" content=\\"script-src 'self'\\">"],[116,"Chrome DevTools is a built-in set of tools for debugging web applications. It includes the console for viewing errors, the Network tab for analysing network requests, Sources for debugging JavaScript and Elements for inspecting the DOM. These tools help developers find and fix errors quickly.","What is Chrome DevTools?\\nChrome DevTools is a set of developer tools built into the Google Chrome browser. It lets you analyse and debug web pages, uncover JavaScript errors, optimize performance and study network requests. DevTools is indispensable to frontend developers when building and maintaining web applications.\\nThe main tools for analysing errors\\n\\n- The Console — displays JavaScript errors, warnings and logs. It lets you execute code in real time.\\n- Sources — provides access to the source code and lets you set breakpoints and step through the code.\\n- Network — shows every network request, its status, loading time and headers. It helps you uncover problems with APIs or resources.\\n- Elements — lets you inspect and edit HTML and CSS in real time, which is useful for checking styles.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose the data on a page is not loading. Open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab and reload the page. You will see a list of requests. If the request to the API is marked in red (status 404 or 500), that indicates an error on the server. An error message with details may appear in the console at the same time.\\n\\nIn the DevTools console you will see the error's text if the request failed. Using the Sources tab, you can set a breakpoint inside the .catch() and check the value of error.\\nConclusion\\nChrome DevTools is the main tool for debugging web applications. Knowing it is mandatory for any frontend developer, since it speeds up the finding and fixing of errors and also helps optimize performance.","// An example of code that may cause an error\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[117,"Testing a date input field involves checking the correct formats (DD.MM.YYYY, for example), boundary values (29 February in a leap year), invalid dates (31 April) and special cases (past/future dates). It is also important to check the handling of an empty field, the entry of characters, and localization. Use both manual and automated testing with various scenarios.","The main aspects of testing a date input field\\nTesting a date input field requires checking a great many scenarios in order to be sure that the user's input is handled correctly. This includes validating the format, boundary values, error handling and conformity with the business logic.\\nChecking the formats and validation\\nMake sure the field accepts only the expected date formats (DD.MM.YYYY or YYYY-MM-DD, for example). Check entry with separators (dots, slashes, dashes) and without them. An example test:\\n\\nBoundary values and special cases\\n\\n- Check 29 February in a leap year and in a non-leap year.\\n- Test 31 April, 30 February and other dates that do not exist.\\n- Include a check of the minimum and maximum permissible date (not earlier than 1900, for example).\\nError handling and UX\\nMake sure that a clear error message is displayed when an incorrect date is entered. Check the behaviour with an empty field and with letters or special characters entered. It is also important to test localization (different date formats in different countries).\\nAutomating the testing\\nFor automation use tools such as Selenium or Cypress. An example test in Cypress:\\n\\nConclusion: testing a date input field is necessary for ensuring the application works correctly, preventing input errors and improving the user experience. It matters especially in systems involving booking, finance or scheduling.","// An example of checking the format in JavaScript\\nfunction isValidDate(dateString) {\\n  const regex = /^\\\\d{2}\\\\.\\\\d{2}\\\\.\\\\d{4}$/;\\n  return regex.test(dateString);\\n}\\nconsole.log(isValidDate('31.12.2023')); // true\\nconsole.log(isValidDate('2023-12-31')); // false\\n\\ncy.get('input[type=\\"date\\"]')\\n  .type('2023-12-31')\\n  .should('have.value', '2023-12-31');\\ncy.get('input[type=\\"date\\"]')\\n  .clear()\\n  .type('invalid-date')\\n  .should('have.class', 'error');"],[118,"Logcat is a utility for viewing Android's system logs. It lets developers track messages from applications, errors and system events. It is used for debugging through Android Studio or the adb command line.","What is logcat?\\nLogcat is a command-line tool and a built-in feature of Android Studio that collects and displays system messages, including application logs, errors, warnings and debugging information. It is necessary for diagnosing problems in an application's operation, such as crashes, memory leaks or incorrect behaviour.\\nHow do you use logcat?\\nLogcat can be launched through Android Studio in the Logcat panel, or through the terminal with the command \`adb logcat\`. You can filter the messages by severity level (VERBOSE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR) and by the tags that are set in the application's code.\\nAn example of use in code\\n\\nConclusion\\nLogcat is an indispensable tool for debugging Android applications, letting you find and fix errors quickly. It is worth using at every stage of development to monitor an application's behaviour.","import android.util.Log;\\n\\npublic class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {\\n    private static final String TAG = \\"MainActivity\\";\\n\\n    @Override\\n    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {\\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);\\n        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);\\n        \\n        Log.d(TAG, \\"Activity created\\");\\n        try {\\n            // some code\\n        } catch (Exception e) {\\n            Log.e(TAG, \\"Error occurred\\", e);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[119,"Testing mobile applications requires allowing for the differences between the operating systems in navigation, permissions, notifications and performance. For iOS, Xcode and the simulator are used; for Android, Android Studio and the emulator. It is important to test on real devices because of the differences in screens, OS versions and hardware characteristics. The different approaches to memory management and power consumption are also taken into account.","The main differences between iOS and Android in testing\\nWhen testing mobile applications you have to allow for the fundamental differences between the iOS and Android platforms. iOS has a closed ecosystem with a limited number of devices, which makes compatibility testing easier but requires strict adherence to Apple's guidelines. Android, by contrast, is open and fragmented: many manufacturers, OS versions and screen sizes. This requires more extensive testing on different devices.\\nTools and approaches\\nFor iOS testing, Xcode with XCTest and the simulator are used, and the simulator does not emulate a real device's behaviour fully (sensors or performance, for example). For Android it is Android Studio with Espresso and the emulator, which imitates real conditions better. It is recommended to combine automated testing (UI tests, unit tests) with manual testing on real devices.\\nExample code for a UI test on iOS (XCTest)\\n\\nExample code for a UI test on Android (Espresso)\\n\\nThe key aspects of testing\\n\\n- Navigation and gestures: iOS uses gestures (swipes, 3D Touch), Android uses buttons and gestures (Back, for example).\\n- Permissions: iOS asks for permissions at the moment of use, Android at installation or while running.\\n- Notifications: different APIs and behaviour (the grouping of notifications, for example).\\n- Performance: iOS is optimized for particular hardware, Android requires testing on various chipsets.\\nConclusion: testing mobile applications has to allow for the platforms' particulars, use specialized tools and always include tests on real devices in order to uncover problems that are invisible in simulators.","import XCTest\\n\\nclass LoginTests: XCTestCase {\\n    let app = XCUIApplication()\\n\\n    override func setUp() {\\n        super.setUp()\\n        continueAfterFailure = false\\n        app.launch()\\n    }\\n\\n    func testLoginSuccess() {\\n        let usernameField = app.textFields[\\"username\\"]\\n        usernameField.tap()\\n        usernameField.typeText(\\"testuser\\")\\n\\n        let passwordField = app.secureTextFields[\\"password\\"]\\n        passwordField.tap()\\n        passwordField.typeText(\\"password123\\")\\n\\n        app.buttons[\\"login\\"].tap()\\n        XCTAssertTrue(app.staticTexts[\\"Welcome\\"].exists)\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimport androidx.test.espresso.Espresso.onView\\nimport androidx.test.espresso.action.ViewActions.*\\nimport androidx.test.espresso.matcher.ViewMatchers.*\\nimport androidx.test.ext.junit.rules.ActivityScenarioRule\\nimport org.junit.Rule\\nimport org.junit.Test\\n\\nclass LoginTest {\\n    @get:Rule\\n    val activityRule = ActivityScenarioRule(LoginActivity::class.java)\\n\\n    @Test\\n    fun testLoginSuccess() {\\n        onView(withId(R.id.username)).perform(typeText(\\"testuser\\"))\\n        onView(withId(R.id.password)).perform(typeText(\\"password123\\"))\\n        onView(withId(R.id.login_button)).perform(click())\\n        onView(withText(\\"Welcome\\")).check(matches(isDisplayed()))\\n    }\\n}"],[120,"To intercept traffic, proxy tools such as Charles Proxy, Fiddler and Wireshark are used. They let you view, modify and analyse HTTP/HTTPS requests and responses. This helps debug APIs and check headers, cookies and request bodies. Charles is especially popular among mobile application developers.","What is traffic interception and what is it for?\\nIntercepting traffic is the process of monitoring and analysing the network requests between a client (a browser, a mobile application) and a server. It is necessary for debugging, security testing, optimizing performance and understanding how an API works.\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- Charles Proxy — a popular tool for macOS and Windows. It lets you intercept HTTP/HTTPS traffic, modify requests and responses in real time, and emulate slow connections.\\n- Fiddler — a powerful proxy for Windows that supports scripting and extended analysis.\\n- Wireshark — a packet analyser for studying network protocols in depth; it works at a low level.\\nAn example of using Charles Proxy\\nSetting Charles up to intercept HTTPS traffic from a mobile device:\\n\\nOnce it is set up you can see every request, its headers, its body and the response status. When debugging a mobile application, for example, you can check what data is being sent to the server and what comes back in response.\\nConclusion\\nTraffic interception tools are indispensable for debugging client-server interaction, especially when developing mobile applications and web services. They help you find errors in an API quickly, check security and optimize requests.","1. Install Charles on your computer.\\n2. Turn on SSL Proxying: Proxy -> SSL Proxying Settings.\\n3. Add an entry for *.* (all hosts).\\n4. Configure the proxy on the device: the computer's IP, port 8888.\\n5. Install the Charles certificate on the device in order to decrypt HTTPS.\\n6. Now every request is displayed in Charles."],[121,"Simulators cannot fully reproduce the hardware characteristics of real devices, such as processor performance or the behaviour of sensors or the battery. They also do not support some hardware-related APIs, the camera or GPS for example. This can mean that bugs related to the real environment go unnoticed. Simulators are therefore good for initial testing but do not replace tests on real devices.","The limitations of simulators\\nSimulators are software tools that imitate the behaviour of a real device or system, but they have a number of limitations that are important to bear in mind during development and testing. The main problem is that simulators cannot reproduce hardware characteristics accurately, such as processor speed, the amount of RAM or the behaviour of sensors (the accelerometer, the gyroscope). This means an application's performance in a simulator may differ greatly from a real device.\\nExamples of the limitations\\n\\n- No support for real hardware APIs: the camera, GPS, Bluetooth or NFC, for example, may be unavailable or work incorrectly.\\n- Differences in network behaviour: simulators often use the host's network, which does not reflect the real conditions of a mobile connection.\\n- Limitations as to battery life: simulators do not emulate power consumption, so you cannot check how an application affects the battery charge.\\nA practical example\\nConsider testing an iOS application using the Xcode simulator. Code for working with the camera might look like this:\\n\\nIn the simulator this code will not be able to access the camera, which can hide errors in the video-handling logic. On a real device the camera will work, but problems with resolution or focus may arise that the simulator will not show.\\nConclusion\\nSimulators are useful for quick prototyping and for testing an application's logic, but to uncover problems related to hardware, performance and real conditions of use, testing on physical devices is necessary. Use simulators for the initial check, but always supplement them with tests on real hardware.","import UIKit\\nimport AVFoundation\\n\\nclass CameraViewController: UIViewController {\\n    var captureSession: AVCaptureSession?\\n    \\n    override func viewDidLoad() {\\n        super.viewDidLoad()\\n        // In the simulator AVCaptureDevice.default(for: .video) will return nil\\n        guard let camera = AVCaptureDevice.default(for: .video) else {\\n            print(\\"The camera is unavailable in the simulator\\")\\n            return\\n        }\\n        // The further configuration of the session\\n    }\\n}"],[122,"To check that markup matches the mockups, tools for overlaying the mockup on top of the page are used — PixelPerfect or PerfectPixel, for example. You can also compare screenshots in Figma or use the browser's built-in tools. It is important to check the spacing, font sizes, colours and the position of elements.","The main methods of checking markup\\nChecking that markup matches the mockups is the process of comparing the finished HTML/CSS code with the design mockup created in Figma, Sketch or Adobe XD. The main goal is to be sure that every element is positioned correctly and that the spacing, dimensions, colours and typography have been observed.\\nTools for checking\\n\\n- PixelPerfect — a browser extension that lets you overlay an image of the mockup on top of the page and adjust its transparency.\\n- PerfectPixel — a similar tool; it supports several layers and automatic alignment.\\n- Figma Mirror — viewing the mockup on a mobile device in order to check responsiveness.\\n- The browser's built-in tools — the element inspector for measuring spacing and dimensions.\\nAn example of using PixelPerfect\\n\\nWhat to check\\n\\n- The spacing (margin/padding) between blocks\\n- Font sizes and line heights\\n- The colours of backgrounds, text and borders\\n- The position of elements relative to one another\\n- Responsiveness at different resolutions\\nConclusion: using tools for pixel-perfect checking helps you spot discrepancies quickly and reduces the time spent on corrections. It is especially useful at the markup stage before handing the project over to the designer or the client.","// Install the PixelPerfect extension for Chrome\\n// Open the page with your markup\\n// Click the extension's icon and upload a screenshot of the mockup\\n// Adjust the transparency and position in order to compare the layers"],[123,"Perfect Pixel is an approach in which a web page's markup matches the design mockup exactly, to the pixel. It is used to achieve a high degree of accuracy in the visual presentation, especially in projects with strict design requirements. To do this, developers use tools for overlaying the mockup on top of the page and check the dimensions, spacing and fonts.","What is Perfect Pixel?\\nPerfect Pixel (or Pixel Perfect) is a markup method in which every element of a web page matches the design mockup (normally in PSD or Figma format) visually, to within a single pixel. This means the dimensions, spacing, colours, fonts and the position of elements all have to be identical to the mockup.\\nHow is it used?\\nThe developer opens the mockup in a graphics editor or uses a special browser extension (PixelPerfect or PerfectPixel, for example) that overlays a semi-transparent image of the mockup on top of the finished page. They then compare the elements and adjust the CSS properties until they match exactly.\\nAn example of the approach\\nSuppose that in the mockup a button is 120x40 pixels in size, with a left margin of 20px and a 14px Arial font. In code it will look like this:\\n\\nOnce the markup is done, the developer switches the extension on, overlays the mockup and checks whether the button matches the image. If it does not, they adjust the values.\\nConclusion\\nPerfect Pixel is useful in projects where the design is strictly prescribed and a high degree of accuracy is required — in landing pages or corporate sites, for example. It can slow development down, however, so it is worth applying selectively, when it is genuinely necessary.",".button {\\n  width: 120px;\\n  height: 40px;\\n  margin-left: 20px;\\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\n  font-size: 14px;\\n}"],[124,"Pixel perfect is an approach in which the markup matches the design mockup exactly. To check it, browser extensions such as PixelPerfect or PerfectPixel are used, which overlay the mockup on top of the page. Tools such as Zeplin and Figma (comparison mode) are also applied, along with automated solutions such as Percy or Applitools.","What pixel perfect is and why it is needed\\nPixel perfect is an approach in frontend development in which the final web page matches the design mockup visually down to every pixel. This matters especially in projects with strict design requirements — in corporate sites or online shops, for example, where the brand has to look consistent.\\nThe main tools for checking\\n\\n- Browser extensions: PerfectPixel (Chrome), PixelPerfect (Firefox) — they let you upload an image of the mockup and overlay it on the page with adjustable transparency.\\n- Design tools: Figma, Zeplin, Avocode — they have built-in comparison modes or export with CSS properties.\\n- Automated services: Percy, Applitools, Chromatic — they perform visual regression testing, comparing screenshots of pages against a reference.\\nAn example of using the PerfectPixel extension\\n\\nWhen to apply pixel perfect\\nThis approach is justified in projects with a fixed design and high demands for accuracy. For responsive or prototype sites, semantic markup without strict pixel control is enough. Conclusion: pixel perfect is a useful technique for a final check but is not always mandatory; use it when an exact match to the mockup is required.","// Install the PerfectPixel extension from the Chrome Web Store\\n// Open the page and click the extension's icon\\n// Upload the PNG mockup and adjust the transparency (usually 50%)\\n// Compare the position of the elements: spacing, font sizes, colours\\n// Edit the CSS in the browser's inspector if necessary"],[125,"When testing you need to take into account the browsers your target audience uses: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Safari, Microsoft Edge and possibly Opera. It is important to check the latest stable versions and also the previous one or two versions of each browser. For mobile devices the mobile versions of Safari and Chrome are added. The decision about supporting old browsers such as Internet Explorer is made on the basis of usage analytics.","Which browsers to take into account when testing\\nThe choice of browsers for testing depends on the target audience and on usage statistics. The main browsers normally included in testing are: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Apple Safari and Microsoft Edge. Opera may also be required and, in rare cases, Internet Explorer (if your audience uses it).\\nThe criteria for choosing\\n\\n- Market share: use analytics data (Google Analytics, StatCounter) to determine which browsers are popular among your users.\\n- Versions: test the latest stable version and the previous one or two versions of each browser.\\n- Mobile browsers: take the mobile versions of Safari (iOS) and Chrome (Android) into account, and also built-in browsers.\\n- Old browsers: if support for old versions is required (IE11, for example), include them in your testing, but this increases the cost.\\nAn example approach\\nFor a web application with a wide audience you can use the following list:\\n\\nIt is also recommended to use tools for emulating old browsers, such as BrowserStack or Sauce Labs.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of browsers for testing should be based on data about your audience and on the project's requirements. The optimum is to test the latest versions of the popular browsers and the mobile versions, in order to ensure a good user experience.","Browsers for testing:\\n- Chrome (the latest version)\\n- Firefox (the latest version)\\n- Safari (the latest version)\\n- Edge (the latest version)\\n- Mobile Safari (iOS)\\n- Mobile Chrome (Android)"],[126,"An interface's responsiveness is tested by emulating devices in the browser, changing the window's dimensions and checking on real devices. The developer tools of Chrome and Firefox and online services are used. It is important to check that the content displays correctly, that the media queries work and that there is no horizontal scrolling.","The main approaches to testing responsiveness\\nTesting an interface's responsiveness is the process of checking how a web page displays and functions on various devices with different screen sizes, resolutions and orientations. The main goal is to be sure that the user interface stays convenient and functional on every device, from mobile phones to wide-format monitors.\\nTools and methods\\n\\n- Emulating devices in the browser: the developer tools of Chrome and Firefox let you emulate various devices and change the screen resolution, the DPI and the orientation. This is a quick way of checking basic responsiveness.\\n- Changing the browser window's dimensions: a simple but effective method is to change the browser window's width by hand in order to see how the interface responds to a change in size.\\n- Real devices: testing on physical devices (smartphones, tablets, laptops) gives the most accurate results, since it takes the particulars of real browsers and operating systems into account.\\n- Online services: services such as BrowserStack, Sauce Labs or LambdaTest provide access to real devices and browsers in the cloud for cross-browser testing.\\nWhat to check\\n\\n- Media queries: be sure that the CSS media queries are applied correctly for the various resolutions.\\n- No horizontal scrolling: the content must not extend beyond the screen horizontally.\\n- The readability of text: the font size and line height have to be comfortable to read.\\n- The behaviour of interactive elements: buttons, links and forms must be reachable by tapping on touch screens.\\n- Images and media: images have to scale and must not be distorted.\\nExample code for checking media queries\\n\\nThis example shows how media queries can be used to change the position of elements depending on the screen's width. When testing, it is important to check that each block of code is applied in the right range of resolutions.\\nConclusion\\nTesting responsiveness is necessary in order to ensure a good user experience on every device. Use a combination of emulation, manual testing and checks on real devices in order to uncover and fix problems before the product is released.","/* CSS media queries for different devices */\\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\\n  .container {\\n    flex-direction: column;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n@media (min-width: 769px) and (max-width: 1024px) {\\n  .container {\\n    flex-direction: row;\\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n@media (min-width: 1025px) {\\n  .container {\\n    flex-direction: row;\\n  }\\n}"],[127,"A breakpoint pauses the execution of code at the place you specify, letting you analyse the program's state step by step. A rewrite replaces the content of an HTTP response or request on the fly without changing the source code. A breakpoint is used to debug logic, while a rewrite is used to test various scenarios for the server's responses.","The main difference\\nA breakpoint and a rewrite are two different tools in the browser's DevTools that solve different problems. A breakpoint pauses the execution of JavaScript at a given line of code, letting you examine variables, the call stack and the application's state. A rewrite (or Overrides) lets you substitute the content of a network request or response without changing the source code on the server.\\nWhen a breakpoint is used\\nA breakpoint is applied for step-by-step debugging: you set a breakpoint in the code and execution stops. You can check the values of variables, run code in the console and step through. This is indispensable when hunting for logical errors, incorrect calculations or unexpected behaviour in functions.\\n\\nWhen a rewrite is used\\nA rewrite lets you override the server's response for a particular URL. You can replace a JSON response, an HTML file or even an image. This is useful for testing different states of the UI without changing the backend: simulating a server error, an empty list or specific data, for example.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- A breakpoint works at the level of code execution, a rewrite at the level of network requests.\\n- A breakpoint requires execution to stop, a rewrite happens transparently to the application.\\n- A breakpoint helps debug logic, a rewrite helps test different data scenarios.\\nConclusion: use a breakpoint to hunt for errors in your code, and a rewrite to check the interface's behaviour with various server responses without changing the backend.","// An example: debugging a function with a breakpoint\\nfunction calculateTotal(items) {\\n  let total = 0;\\n  for (let item of items) {\\n    total += item.price * item.quantity; // we set a breakpoint here\\n  }\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\n// An example: overriding an API response\\n// In DevTools -> Network -> right-click the request -> Override content\\n// We substitute the response with:\\n{\\n  \\"users\\": [],\\n  \\"error\\": \\"Service unavailable\\"\\n}"],[128,"When testing mobile applications it is important to take system events into account, such as incoming calls, SMS messages, notifications, the loss or switching of the network, a change of screen orientation, a low battery charge and the device being locked. These events can interrupt an application's operation, cause data loss or leave it in an incorrect state. Testing should check how the application recovers from such interruptions and preserves the user's data.","Why system events have to be taken into account\\nSystem events are external influences on a mobile device that can interrupt an application's normal operation. Neglecting to test them leads to crashes, data loss and a poor user experience. For example, if an application does not handle an incoming call, it may freeze or lose the data that was entered.\\nThe main kinds of system event\\n\\n- Incoming calls and SMS messages — they interrupt the current activity; the application has to pause and recover properly once the call has ended.\\n- Notifications — push notifications or system alerts may cover the interface; it is important to check that the application does not lose its state.\\n- Loss of network — going offline; the application has to store data locally and synchronize when the connection is restored.\\n- A change of orientation — rotating the screen may cause the Activity (Android) or ViewController (iOS) to be recreated; the state has to be preserved.\\n- A low battery charge — the system may restrict background processes; the application has to finish its operations properly.\\n- The screen being locked — the application goes into the background; it is important to preserve progress and prevent memory leaks.\\nAn example of testing on Android\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\nSeparate test cases have to be written for every system event. Use emulators or real devices to simulate the events (through ADB commands for Android, for example). Automate the checks with frameworks such as Espresso or XCTest in order to guarantee stability with every build.\\nConclusion: taking system events into account is mandatory for ensuring a mobile application's reliability. It prevents data loss, improves the user experience and increases the application's rating in the stores.","// An example of handling an incoming call in an Activity\\n@Override\\nprotected void onPause() {\\n    super.onPause();\\n    // We save the current state\\n    saveState();\\n}\\n\\n@Override\\nprotected void onResume() {\\n    super.onResume();\\n    // We restore the state after the call\\n    restoreState();\\n}"],[129,"To check an interface against the mockups, visual comparison (pixel perfect), tools for automated regression testing (Percy, Chromatic) and a manual review are used. It is important to check the spacing, the fonts, the colours and the behaviour at different resolutions. This helps avoid discrepancies between the design and the implementation.","Checking an interface against the mockups\\nThe correct display of an interface against the mockups is a key stage of development that guarantees the user sees exactly what the designer intended. Both manual and automated methods are applied for this.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Pixel-perfect comparison — overlaying a screenshot of the page on the mockup with some transparency in order to uncover discrepancies.\\n- Visual regression testing — automatically comparing screenshots against reference ones with tools (Percy, Chromatic, Applitools).\\n- A manual review — checking the spacing, fonts, colours and responsiveness by hand with the browser's developer tools.\\nExample code for visual testing with Percy\\n\\nConclusion\\nChecking an interface against the mockups regularly helps maintain the quality of the UI and prevents visual defects from accumulating. Automation is especially useful in projects where the design changes often.","// An example of integrating Percy with Cypress\\ncy.visit('/page');\\ncy.percySnapshot('Homepage'); // Takes a screenshot and compares it with the reference"],[130,"To check an interface's responsiveness you can use the browsers' built-in developer tools (Chrome DevTools, Firefox Developer Tools), which let you emulate various devices and screen resolutions. There are also online services such as Responsinator and BrowserStack, and Lighthouse for an automated audit. In addition you can use physical devices and simulators for more accurate testing.","Tools for checking an interface's responsiveness\\nAn interface's responsiveness is a web page's ability to display correctly on various devices with different screen sizes. There are several categories of tool for checking it, from those built into the browser to specialized services.\\nThe browser's developer tools\\nThe most accessible way is to use the browser's developer tools. In Chrome DevTools, for example, there is a device emulation mode (Toggle Device Toolbar) that lets you pick a particular device or set an arbitrary resolution. You can check how media queries affect the layout and simulate touch events.\\n\\nOnline services and automated tools\\n\\n- Responsinator — a quick check on popular devices.\\n- BrowserStack — lets you test on real devices and browsers in the cloud.\\n- Lighthouse — a tool built into Chrome for auditing performance and accessibility, including a check of responsiveness.\\nPhysical devices and simulators\\nFor accurate testing it is recommended to use real smartphones and tablets. If you do not have them, the iOS (Xcode Simulator) and Android (Android Studio Emulator) simulators will do. They give a more realistic picture than emulation in the browser.\\nConclusion\\nUse a combination of the browser's tools for quick iterative checks, online services for cross-browser testing and physical devices for the final check. This will ensure the interface displays correctly on every target device.","/* An example of a CSS media query */\\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\\n  .container {\\n    flex-direction: column;\\n  }\\n}"],[131,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight format based on key-value pairs that is easy to read and parse. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is a heavier markup language with tags and attributes that supports schemas and namespaces. JSON is normally simpler and faster for web APIs, whereas XML is more often used in complex documents and in systems with strict schemas.","The main differences between JSON and XML\\nJSON and XML are two popular formats for storing and transmitting structured data. JSON came out of JavaScript and became the standard for web APIs thanks to its simplicity. XML, by contrast, is an older, more powerful markup language that is widely applied in configuration files, documents and systems where strict validation matters.\\nSyntax and readability\\nJSON uses a minimalist syntax: objects in curly braces, arrays in square ones, the data represented as key-value pairs. XML uses opening and closing tags, which makes it more cumbersome. An example:\\n\\nData types and support\\nJSON supports a limited set of types: strings, numbers, booleans, arrays, objects and null. XML has no built-in types — all the data is strings, which requires additional handling. JSON is easier to parse in programming languages, especially in JavaScript, where it can be turned into an object with \`JSON.parse()\`.\\nUse\\nJSON dominates in RESTful APIs, in configuration (package.json, for example) and in web applications. XML is often used in SOAP services, SVG graphics, RSS feeds and systems that require a strict schema (XSD).\\nConclusion\\nThe choice between JSON and XML depends on the task: for simple, fast exchanges of data on the web, JSON suits better, while for complex documents with validation and namespaces, XML does.","// JSON\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"city\\": \\"New York\\"\\n}\\n\\n// XML\\n<person>\\n  <name>Alice</name>\\n  <age>30</age>\\n  <city>New York</city>\\n</person>"],[132,"DevTools are the browser's built-in tools for debugging. To analyse markup, use the Elements tab, where you can inspect and change the HTML/CSS in real time. For network requests, open the Network tab, where you can see every request, its status, its duration and its headers. This helps you find problems with display or with loading data quickly.","The basics of working with DevTools\\nDevTools (the developer tools) are a powerful set of tools built into modern browsers such as Chrome, Firefox and Edge. They let developers analyse, debug and optimize web pages. To open them, the F12 key or the combination Ctrl+Shift+I (Cmd+Option+I on a Mac) is normally used.\\nAnalysing markup with the Elements tab\\nThe Elements tab (or Inspector) shows the page's DOM tree and its CSS styles. You can:\\n\\n- Hover over elements in the tree in order to highlight them on the page.\\n- Edit the HTML and CSS in real time — the changes are visible at once but are not saved.\\n- Inspect the computed styles, the dimensions of blocks (the box model) and the CSS rules that have been applied.\\nAn example: if an element has the wrong spacing, you can find it in Elements, look at the box model and change the margin or padding right there in the Styles panel.\\nAnalysing network requests with the Network tab\\nThe Network tab shows every HTTP request the page makes: loading the HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images, API requests and so on. Useful capabilities:\\n\\n- Viewing the response status (200, 404, 500 and so on).\\n- The duration of every request (the time spent waiting, loading).\\n- The request's and response's headers, including Content-Type and Cache-Control.\\n- The response body (JSON or HTML, for example).\\nAn example: if the page is not loading its data, open Network, find the request to the API and check its status and response body. If the status is 404, the URL is probably wrong.\\nA practical example\\n\\nConclusion\\nDevTools are an indispensable tool for every web developer. Use Elements for quickly debugging markup and Network for diagnosing problems with loading resources and with API requests. This speeds development up and helps you find errors without reloading the page.","// An example: checking that data loads through fetch\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));\\n\\n// In the DevTools Network tab you will see this request, its status and its duration.\\n// If the request is not shown, check the filters (XHR, for example)."],[133,"Compatibility testing is a kind of non-functional testing that checks how an application works in different environments: browsers, operating systems, devices and software versions. The goal is to be sure the product functions correctly for every user. A site, for example, should display the same way in Chrome, Firefox and Safari.","What is compatibility testing?\\nCompatibility testing is the process of checking that software works correctly in given combinations of hardware and software. It helps uncover problems that users may encounter because of differences in their environment.\\nThe main aspects\\n\\n- Browsers: checking in Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and others.\\n- Operating systems: Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, Android.\\n- Devices: different screen resolutions, mobile phones, tablets.\\n- Software versions: old and new versions of browsers and libraries.\\nExample code for checking compatibility\\n\\nCode like this lets you adapt your styles according to the browser's capabilities.\\nConclusion\\nCompatibility testing is necessary in order to ensure an application works reliably on every target platform. It matters especially for web applications, where users have widely differing environments.","// Checking support for CSS Grid in the browser\\nif (typeof CSS !== 'undefined' && CSS.supports('display', 'grid')) {\\n  console.log('CSS Grid is supported');\\n} else {\\n  console.log('CSS Grid is not supported, use a fallback');\\n}"],[134,"An HTTP status code is a three-digit number that the server sends in response to a request from a browser or another client. It shows whether the request succeeded, whether an error occurred or whether an additional action is required. The codes fall into categories: 1xx (informational), 2xx (success), 3xx (redirection), 4xx (client error) and 5xx (server error). For example, 200 means success, 404 means the page was not found, and 500 means an internal server error.","What is an HTTP status code?\\nAn HTTP status code is a three-digit integer that the server returns in response to a client's request (from a browser or a mobile application, for example). It is part of the first line of the HTTP response and reports the result of processing the request. The codes are standardized in the HTTP specification and fall into five classes, each of which begins with a particular digit.\\nThe categories of status code\\n\\n- 1xx (Informational): informational codes indicating that the request has been accepted and is being processed. 101 Switching Protocols, for example, is used when upgrading to WebSocket.\\n- 2xx (Success): success codes meaning that the request was successfully received, understood and processed. The most widespread is 200 OK.\\n- 3xx (Redirection): redirection codes indicating that the client has to perform an additional action in order to complete the request. 301 Moved Permanently, for example, means the resource has moved to a new URL.\\n- 4xx (Client Error): errors on the client's side, such as incorrect request syntax or a missing resource. For example, 404 Not Found means the page was not found; 401 Unauthorized means authorization is required.\\n- 5xx (Server Error): errors on the server's side, when the server cannot fulfil a valid request. 500 Internal Server Error, for example, is an internal server error.\\nAn example of use in code\\nWhen developing web applications in Node.js with Express you can set the status codes explicitly:\\n\\nIn this example, if the user is not found, the server returns the code 404 with an error message. If the user exists, it returns 200 and the data.\\nConclusion\\nThe HTTP status code is a fundamental tool for understanding and debugging the interaction between client and server. Knowing the categories and the typical codes helps you diagnose problems in web applications quickly and handle responses correctly on the client side.","const express = require('express');\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.get('/user/:id', (req, res) => {\\n  const userId = req.params.id;\\n  const user = findUserById(userId);\\n  if (!user) {\\n    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });\\n  }\\n  res.status(200).json(user);\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);"],[135,"A mobile application's operation is affected by the device's hardware limitations, such as the processor, the memory and the battery. The network connection, the operating system's version and device fragmentation also matter. Optimizing the code, working with graphics and managing memory directly affect responsiveness and stability.","The main factors affecting a mobile application's operation\\nA mobile application's performance depends on a great many aspects, from the device's hardware characteristics to the quality of the network connection. Understanding these factors helps developers build more stable, faster applications.\\nHardware limitations\\n\\n- The processor and the memory: different devices have different computing power and amounts of RAM. An application has to use the resources efficiently, avoiding memory leaks and excessive load on the CPU.\\n- The battery: power consumption is critical for mobile devices. Background processes, frequent network requests and heavy graphics can drain a battery quickly.\\n- The screen and graphics: the screen's resolution and the GPU's capabilities affect the rendering of the interface. Unoptimized images and animations can cause lag.\\nSoftware and network factors\\n\\n- The operating system and its version: different versions of iOS and Android have different APIs and limitations. An application has to be compatible with a wide range of versions.\\n- The network connection: network latency, packet loss and an unreliable connection affect the loading of data. Caching and the handling of network errors have to be implemented.\\n- Device fragmentation: a great many models with different characteristics require thorough testing and adaptation of the interface.\\nAn example of optimizing the loading of images\\n\\nIn this example the Glide library manages caching and the loading of images on a background thread automatically, which reduces the load on the UI and saves data.\\nConclusion\\nAllowing for hardware limitations, optimizing network interaction and testing on various devices are key to building a responsive, energy-efficient mobile application. Applying these practices matters especially when developing applications for the mass market with a wide range of devices.","// An example of caching images on Android using Glide\\nImageView imageView = findViewById(R.id.image_view);\\nString imageUrl = \\"https://example.com/image.jpg\\";\\n\\nGlide.with(this)\\n    .load(imageUrl)\\n    .placeholder(R.drawable.placeholder)\\n    .error(R.drawable.error)\\n    .diskCacheStrategy(DiskCacheStrategy.ALL)\\n    .into(imageView);"],[136,"The Network tab in DevTools shows every network request a page makes: loading times, statuses, headers. The Response tab displays the body of the server's response in various formats (JSON, HTML, an image). This helps you debug APIs, check data and optimize loading.","What are the Network and Response tabs?\\nThe Network tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) is a powerful tool for analysing all the HTTP requests a web page performs. It shows every request to the server, including static files (CSS, JS, images) and API calls. The Response tab is part of Network and displays the content the server returned for a particular request.\\nHow to use Network for debugging\\nOpen DevTools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I) and go to the Network tab. Reload the page or perform an action. You will see a list of requests. The main columns are:\\n\\n- Name — the name of the file or the request's URL\\n- Status — the HTTP status (200, 404, 500 and so on)\\n- Type — the kind of content (document, script, fetch)\\n- Size — the size of the response\\n- Time — the duration\\nClick any request to see the details: Headers, Preview, Response (the response body) and Timing (the duration of the stages).\\nAn example of analysing a response\\nSuppose you make a request to an API and expect JSON. In the Response tab you will see:\\n\\nIf the response is empty or contains an error, you will notice it at once. You can also check the response headers — Content-Type: application/json, for example.\\nA practical example with code\\nSuppose you use fetch to get the data:\\n\\nOpen Network and find the request /api/users. Check the status (it should be 200), the type of response (JSON) and the response body. If the status is 404, check the URL. If it is 500, the problem is on the server.\\nConclusion\\nThe Network and Response tabs are an indispensable tool for debugging network interaction. They help you find problems with an API quickly, check that the data is correct and optimize the page's loading performance.","{\\n  \\"users\\": [\\n    {\\"id\\": 1, \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\"},\\n    {\\"id\\": 2, \\"name\\": \\"Bob\\"}\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\nfetch('/api/users')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[137,"Describe the bug by giving its title, the environment (browser, OS, screen resolution), the steps to reproduce it, and the actual and expected results. Attach a screenshot or a video. State the priority and the severity of the problem. A clear description helps the developer understand and fix the error quickly.","The structure of a markup bug description\\nFor a markup defect to be fixed effectively, the developer has to be given exhaustive information. The description should be clear, reproducible and contain every key detail.\\nThe main components of a bug report\\n\\n- The title: briefly reflects the essence of the problem — \\"The 'Submit' button extends beyond its container on mobile devices\\", for example.\\n- The environment: give the browser (Chrome 120, Firefox 121), the operating system (Windows 11, macOS 14), the screen resolution (375x812 for an iPhone X) and the device (iPhone 14, Samsung Galaxy S23).\\n- The steps to reproduce: a numbered list of the actions that lead to the bug. For example: 1. Open the page /profile. 2. Click the \\"Edit\\" button. 3. Reduce the window's width to 375px.\\n- The actual result: what really happens. For example: \\"The 'Save' button is overlapped by the text of the input field\\".\\n- The expected result: how it should be. For example: \\"The 'Save' button sits below the input field with 16px of spacing\\".\\n- Attachments: a screenshot or screen recording demonstrating the problem. You can add the HTML/CSS code if it helps localize the problem.\\nAn example description\\n\\nConclusion: describing a markup bug properly saves the team's time, reduces the number of clarifying questions and speeds up the fixing of the defect. Always state the environment and attach visual evidence.","Title: The logo overlaps the menu in Safari on an iPad\\nEnvironment: Safari 17, iPadOS 17, iPad Pro 11\\" (resolution 834x1194)\\nSteps to reproduce:\\n1. Open the home page.\\n2. Rotate the device into landscape orientation.\\nActual result: The logo overlaps the first menu item.\\nExpected result: The logo and the menu are displayed with 20px of spacing.\\nPriority: High (it blocks navigation)."],[138,"The parameters in a GET request are passed in the query string (the URL) after the question mark (?). They have the form key=value and are separated by the & character. For example: /api/users?page=2&limit=10. This is the standard way of passing data in GET requests, used for filtering, sorting and pagination.","How parameters are passed in a GET request\\nIn an HTTP GET request the parameters are passed in the URL (Uniform Resource Locator) string after the question mark (?). This part of the URL is called the query string. The parameters have the form key=value, and several parameters are separated by the & character.\\nAn example of the structure of a URL with parameters\\nConsider the URL: \`https://api.example.com/users?page=2&limit=10&sort=name\`. Here:\\n\\n- \`https://api.example.com/users\` — the base path to the resource\\n- \`?\` — the separator marking the start of the parameters\\n- \`page=2\` — the first parameter (the page number)\\n- \`&\` — the separator between parameters\\n- \`limit=10\` — the second parameter (the number of items per page)\\n- \`sort=name\` — the third parameter (sorting by name)\\nA practical example in JavaScript\\n\\nThe particulars of passing parameters in a GET request\\n\\n- Visibility: the parameters are visible in the URL, so do not use GET to pass confidential data (passwords, tokens).\\n- A length limit: a URL has a length limit (usually 2048 characters), so GET is not suitable for passing large volumes of data.\\n- Caching: GET requests with parameters can be cached by browsers and proxy servers, which speeds up repeat requests.\\n- Idempotence: GET requests must not change the server's state (only read data).\\nConclusion\\nThe parameters in a GET request are passed through the query string in the URL. This method is convenient for filtering, sorting and paginating data, but it is not suitable for passing sensitive information or large volumes of data. Use GET with parameters for safe, idempotent requests that read data.","// Composing a GET request with parameters\\nconst baseUrl = 'https://api.example.com/users';\\nconst params = {\\n  page: 2,\\n  limit: 10,\\n  sort: 'name'\\n};\\n\\n// Converting the object into a query string\\nconst queryString = Object.entries(params)\\n  .map(([key, value]) => \`\${encodeURIComponent(key)}=\${encodeURIComponent(value)}\`)\\n  .join('&');\\n\\nconst fullUrl = \`\${baseUrl}?\${queryString}\`;\\nconsole.log(fullUrl);\\n// https://api.example.com/users?page=2&limit=10&sort=name\\n\\n// Performing the request\\nfetch(fullUrl)\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data));"],[139,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for storing and transmitting data. It consists of \\"key-value\\" pairs, where the keys are strings in double quotes and the values may be strings, numbers, objects, arrays, booleans or null. The data is organized into objects (in curly braces) and arrays (in square brackets).","The basics of JSON's structure\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on JavaScript's syntax. It is used to transmit structured data between server and client, and also to store configuration. JSON's structure consists of two main constructs: the object and the array.\\nAn object\\nAn object is an unordered set of \\"key-value\\" pairs enclosed in curly braces {}. The keys are always strings in double quotes, and the values may be of any permitted type. For example:\\n\\nAn array\\nAn array is an ordered list of values enclosed in square brackets []. The values are separated by commas. For example:\\n\\nThe permitted value types\\n\\n- A string (in double quotes)\\n- A number (an integer or a floating-point one)\\n- A boolean (true or false)\\n- null\\n- An object\\n- An array\\nAn example of a complex structure\\n\\nIn this example the user object contains a string, a number, an array and null. The nesting can go to any depth.\\nConclusion\\nJSON is a simple, readable format that most programming languages parse easily. It is applied for APIs, configuration files and storing data, where universality and minimal size are required.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"isStudent\\": false\\n}\\n\\n[\\n  \\"apple\\",\\n  \\"banana\\",\\n  \\"cherry\\"\\n]\\n\\n{\\n  \\"user\\": {\\n    \\"id\\": 1,\\n    \\"name\\": \\"Bob\\",\\n    \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", \\"coding\\"],\\n    \\"address\\": null\\n  },\\n  \\"active\\": true\\n}"],[140,"The Application tab in DevTools is used to inspect and manage all the data stored in the browser: local storage (localStorage), session storage (sessionStorage), cookies, the cache (Cache Storage) and IndexedDB, and also to manage Service Workers and PWA manifests. It is a key tool for debugging client-side storage and checking that a web application works correctly.","The purpose of the Application tab\\nThe Application tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) provides a centralized interface for inspecting, editing and deleting all the data a web application stores on the client side. It is necessary for debugging work with storage, checking that cookies are set correctly, analysing caching and managing Service Workers.\\nThe main sections\\n\\n- Local Storage — data stored in the browser with no expiry, available within a single origin.\\n- Session Storage — data that is deleted once the tab is closed.\\n- Cookies — small fragments of data sent with every HTTP request, often used for sessions and tracking.\\n- IndexedDB — a more complex client-side database for storing large volumes of structured data.\\n- Cache Storage — part of the Cache API, used to cache resources (in Service Workers, for example).\\n- Service Workers — managing the background scripts that intercept network requests and implement offline functionality.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you want to check whether an authorization token is being saved correctly in localStorage. You can open the Application tab, select Local Storage, find the key 'token' and see its value. You can also change or delete it right there in the interface for testing.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Application tab is an indispensable tool for debugging client-side storage and managing a PWA's resources. It lets you check and modify data quickly, which speeds up the development and testing of web applications.","// An example of saving a token in localStorage\\nlocalStorage.setItem('token', 'abc123');\\n\\n// Checking in DevTools: Application -> Local Storage -> your domain -> token = 'abc123'"],[141,"The Sources tab in Chrome DevTools is used to debug JavaScript code. You can set breakpoints, inspect and change the values of variables, step through the code and analyse the call stack. It is the main tool for finding and fixing errors in client-side code.","The purpose of the Sources tab\\nThe Sources tab in Chrome DevTools is a full-featured environment for debugging JavaScript, CSS and HTML. It lets a developer pause the execution of code at any moment, examine the application's state and step through scripts. It is an indispensable tool for understanding how code works and for hunting down bugs.\\nThe main capabilities\\n\\n- Setting breakpoints: you can click a line number in the editor in order to set a breakpoint. When execution reaches that line, it pauses.\\n- Stepping through: once execution has paused you can use the Step Over (F10), Step Into (F11) and Step Out (Shift+F11) buttons to run the code one line or one function at a time.\\n- Inspecting variables: the Scope panel on the right displays every local, closure and global variable with its current value. You can change them while debugging.\\n- Call Stack: shows the chain of function calls that led to the current breakpoint. This helps you understand where the function was called from.\\n- Watch: lets you add expressions whose values will be computed and displayed at every step of the debugging.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you have a function that does not return the expected result:\\n\\nTo debug it, open the Sources tab, find the file with this code and click the line number of \`let total = price + tax;\`. Then run the code again (reload the page, for example). Execution will stop on that line. In the Scope panel you will see the values \`price = 100\` and \`tax = 20\`. By pressing Step Over you will move to \`return total;\` and see that \`total = 120\`. If the value were wrong, you would notice the problem at once.\\nConclusion\\nThe Sources tab is the main tool for debugging frontend code. It lets a developer not merely read logs but explore a program's execution interactively, which speeds up the finding and fixing of errors considerably. It is worth applying whenever the code's behaviour differs from what you expect, especially in complex scenarios involving asynchrony or a great many function calls.","function calculateTotal(price, tax) {\\n  let total = price + tax;\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\nlet result = calculateTotal(100, 20);\\nconsole.log(result); // 120 is expected, but there may be an error"],[142,"Debounce is a technique that postpones the execution of a function until a certain time has passed since the last call. For a search field this means the request is sent only after the user has stopped typing. It reduces the number of requests to the server and improves performance.","What debounce is and what it is for\\nDebounce is a technique used to limit how often a function is called. It guarantees that the function will run only after a certain time has passed since the last call. This is especially useful for a search field, where every character typed may initiate a request to the server. Without debounce every key pressed would send a request, which leads to an unnecessary load and a poor user experience.\\nHow do you implement debounce?\\nImplementing debounce involves creating a function that takes another function and a delay. It returns a new function which, on every call, resets the timer and sets a new one. When the timer expires, the original function is called.\\n\\nWhere is debounce applied?\\nDebounce is widely used in web development to handle events that can occur often, such as typing text, resizing the window or scrolling. It helps reduce the load on the browser and the server, improving performance.\\nConclusion\\nDebounce is a simple, effective way of optimizing frequent function calls. It is worth applying in any scenario where you need to react to user input or other frequent events, in order to avoid redundant computation and requests.","function debounce(func, delay) {\\n  let timeoutId;\\n  return function(...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId);\\n    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => func.apply(this, args), delay);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// Use for a search field\\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById('search');\\nconst handleSearch = debounce((event) => {\\n  console.log('Search query:', event.target.value);\\n  // Sending the request to the server\\n}, 300);\\n\\nsearchInput.addEventListener('input', handleSearch);"],[143,"Yes, when a parent node matches, all its child nodes are normally displayed too, in order to preserve the integrity of the hierarchy. This lets the user see the full context of the element that was found. The exception is when only an exact match without the nested data is required.","The general concept\\nWhen searching in tree structures (the DOM tree, a file system or hierarchical menus, for example) the question often arises: should the child nodes be shown if the parent matched the search criterion? The answer depends on the context, but in most cases it is yes — the child nodes are displayed, in order to preserve the hierarchy and the context.\\nWhy this matters\\nIf you hide the child nodes when the parent matches, the user may lose their understanding of the structure. In a file manager, for example, when searching for the folder 'Documents' the user expects to see its contents rather than simply an empty folder. In interfaces with trees (a TreeView, for example) this is the standard behaviour.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nThe exceptions\\n\\n- If an exact match without context is required (searching by ID, for example).\\n- In large trees you can display only the parent with an indicator that it has children, for performance.\\nConclusion\\nDisplaying the child nodes when the parent matches improves the user experience and preserves the context. It is the standard practice in interfaces with hierarchical data, unless there are special requirements for the precision of the search.","// A function for searching a tree that displays the child nodes\\nfunction searchTree(node, query) {\\n  if (node.name.includes(query)) {\\n    // We show the parent and all its children\\n    node.visible = true;\\n    node.children.forEach(child => showAllChildren(child));\\n  } else {\\n    // We search in the child nodes\\n    node.children.forEach(child => searchTree(child, query));\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction showAllChildren(node) {\\n  node.visible = true;\\n  node.children.forEach(child => showAllChildren(child));\\n}"],[144,"The main browser engines are: Blink (Chrome, Opera, Edge), WebKit (Safari) and Gecko (Firefox). There are also Trident (the old Internet Explorer) and its successor EdgeHTML (the old Edge). Every engine is responsible for painting HTML and CSS and for executing JavaScript.","The main browser engines\\nA browser engine is the software component responsible for loading, analysing and displaying web pages. It turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into a visual representation on the screen. Knowing the engines helps developers understand why the same code may behave differently in different browsers.\\nA list of the main engines\\n\\n- Blink — used in Google Chrome, Opera, Microsoft Edge (from 2020 onwards), Brave and other Chromium-based browsers. It is a fork of WebKit created by Google in 2013.\\n- WebKit — used in Safari (Apple) and formerly in Chrome. Developed by Apple on the basis of KHTML.\\n- Gecko — used in Firefox (Mozilla). Known for its strict support of web standards.\\n- Trident — the old engine of Internet Explorer (IE4–IE11). No longer supported.\\n- EdgeHTML — used in the old Microsoft Edge (until 2020). It has now been replaced by Blink.\\nExample code for detecting the engine\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding browser engines is necessary for testing and ensuring the compatibility of web applications. Different engines may interpret CSS properties or JavaScript APIs differently, so it is important to check that a site works in several browsers.","// Detecting the browser engine through navigator.userAgent\\nconst userAgent = navigator.userAgent;\\nif (userAgent.includes('Chrome') && !userAgent.includes('Edg')) {\\n  console.log('Blink (Chrome)');\\n} else if (userAgent.includes('Safari') && !userAgent.includes('Chrome')) {\\n  console.log('WebKit (Safari)');\\n} else if (userAgent.includes('Firefox')) {\\n  console.log('Gecko (Firefox)');\\n} else if (userAgent.includes('Edg')) {\\n  console.log('Blink (Edge)');\\n} else {\\n  console.log('Unknown engine');\\n}"],[145,"Cross-browser compatibility is a site's ability to display correctly in every popular browser. The differences arise from different rendering engines (Blink, WebKit, Gecko), incomplete support for standards and obsolete browsers. To ensure compatibility, vendor prefixes, polyfills and progressive enhancement are used.","What is cross-browser compatibility?\\nCross-browser compatibility is the property of a website or application working correctly and displaying the same way in every modern browser, including Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and Opera. It is an important aspect of web development, since users use different browsers and each of them may interpret the code in its own way.\\nWhy do the differences arise?\\nThe main reasons for differences in display:\\n\\n- Different rendering engines: Chrome and Edge use Blink, Firefox uses Gecko, Safari uses WebKit. Each engine handles CSS and JavaScript in its own way.\\n- Incomplete support for standards: new HTML, CSS and JavaScript capabilities may not be implemented in every browser at the same time.\\n- Obsolete browsers: old versions (Internet Explorer, for example) do not support modern technologies.\\n- Vendor prefixes: some CSS properties require prefixes for particular browsers (\`-webkit-\` for Safari, for example).\\nExample code with vendor prefixes\\n\\nHow do you ensure cross-browser compatibility?\\nTo achieve cross-browser compatibility developers use:\\n\\n- Polyfills — scripts that add support for missing features (\`fetch\` for older browsers, for example).\\n- CSS normalization — resetting the browser's default styles (Normalize.css, for example).\\n- Progressive enhancement — creating a basic version that works everywhere and adding improvements for modern browsers.\\n- Testing — checking in different browsers with tools such as BrowserStack.\\nConclusion: cross-browser compatibility is necessary in order to give every user equal access to your content. Apply polyfills, prefixes and testing in order to minimize the differences and improve the user experience.",".box {\\n  display: -webkit-flex; /* Safari */\\n  display: flex; /* The standard */\\n  -webkit-border-radius: 5px; /* Safari */\\n  border-radius: 5px; /* The standard */\\n}"],[146,"Reflow is the process of recalculating the dimensions and positions of the elements on a page when the DOM or the CSS changes. Repaint is the updating of elements' appearance without any change to their geometry. A reflow always causes a repaint, but not the other way round. Frequent reflows reduce performance, so people try to minimize them.","What are reflow and repaint?\\nReflow (or layout) is the process in which the browser recalculates the geometry of elements: their dimensions, positions and spacing. It happens when the DOM changes, when CSS properties that affect dimensions change (width, height, margin, padding), or when the window is resized. Repaint is the process of updating the pixels on the screen without any change to the geometry — when a background or text colour changes, for example.\\nHow are they connected?\\nA reflow always causes a repaint, since after the geometry has been recalculated the appearance has to be updated. A repaint can happen without a reflow if only visual properties change (color, visibility, background-color).\\nCode examples\\n\\nHow do you minimize reflows?\\n\\n- Change classes rather than individual styles.\\n- Work with elements outside the flow (position: absolute/fixed).\\n- Use a documentFragment for bulk changes to the DOM.\\n- Avoid reading properties that cause a reflow often (offsetHeight, clientWidth).\\nConclusion: understanding reflow and repaint helps you write fast web applications, especially with animations and dynamic content. Minimizing reflows is the key to a smooth interface.","// Causes a reflow and a repaint\\ndocument.getElementById('box').style.width = '200px';\\n\\n// Causes only a repaint\\ndocument.getElementById('box').style.backgroundColor = 'red';\\n\\n// Reading offsetHeight causes a reflow (a forced layout)\\nconst height = document.getElementById('box').offsetHeight;"],[147,"The getBoundingClientRect method returns a DOMRect object with the element's coordinates relative to the browser window (the viewport). It contains the properties top, right, bottom, left, width and height. This is useful for determining whether an element is visible, for animations or for positioning tooltips.","What is getBoundingClientRect?\\nThe \`getBoundingClientRect\` method is called on a DOM element and returns a \`DOMRect\` object describing the element's size and its position relative to the visible area of the window (the viewport). The coordinates are measured from the top left corner of the viewport, not of the document as a whole.\\nThe main properties of the returned object\\n\\n- \`top\` — the distance from the top edge of the viewport to the top edge of the element.\\n- \`bottom\` — the distance from the top edge of the viewport to the bottom edge of the element.\\n- \`left\` — the distance from the left edge of the viewport to the left edge of the element.\\n- \`right\` — the distance from the left edge of the viewport to the right edge of the element.\\n- \`width\` and \`height\` — the element's width and height, including its padding and border.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Checking whether an element is visible on the screen (for the lazy loading of images, for example).\\n- Positioning pop-ups or tooltips relative to an element.\\n- Animations that depend on an element's position (parallax, for example).\\nConclusion\\nThe \`getBoundingClientRect\` method is a simple, effective way of getting an element's exact coordinates in the viewport. It is indispensable for tasks involving the determination of visibility and positioning in dynamic interfaces.","const element = document.getElementById('myBox');\\nconst rect = element.getBoundingClientRect();\\nconsole.log(rect.top, rect.left, rect.width, rect.height);\\n// For example: 100, 50, 200, 150"],[148,"Transform uses the GPU to move an element without changing its geometry, which triggers only compositing. Position changes an element's location through the top/left properties, which triggers a layout recalculation (reflow) and a repaint, loading the CPU. Transform works faster and more smoothly, especially in animation.","The main difference between transform and position in animation\\nThe \`transform\` property and the \`top\`/\`left\`/\`right\`/\`bottom\` properties (through \`position\`) affect the browser's rendering process differently. \`transform\` works at the compositing stage without touching layout and paint, which makes it more performant for animations.\\nHow it works\\nWhen \`position\` changes (\`left: 100px\`, for example) the browser runs the full cycle: recalculating the layout (reflow), then a repaint and only then compositing. This loads the CPU. \`transform\`, by contrast, moves the element on a separate layer using the GPU, bypassing layout and paint. This is especially noticeable with frequent changes (60fps).\\nA code example\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- transform — for animations of movement, scaling and rotation. It provides smoothness and high performance.\\n- position — for static positioning or infrequent changes, where performance is not critical.\\nConclusion: for animations always prefer \`transform\`, since it uses the GPU and does not cause the layout to be recalculated, which is critical for the smoothness of interfaces.","/* A slow animation through position */\\n.box {\\n  position: relative;\\n  animation: move 2s infinite;\\n}\\n@keyframes move {\\n  from { left: 0; }\\n  to { left: 200px; }\\n}\\n\\n/* A fast animation through transform */\\n.box {\\n  animation: moveTransform 2s infinite;\\n}\\n@keyframes moveTransform {\\n  from { transform: translateX(0); }\\n  to { transform: translateX(200px); }\\n}"],[149,"The CSSOM (CSS Object Model) is the object model of CSS, analogous to the DOM for HTML. The browser builds the CSSOM from the CSS rules and then combines it with the DOM into the render tree in order to paint the page. Without the CSSOM the browser does not know how to style elements, which is why building the CSSOM blocks rendering.","What is the CSSOM?\\nThe CSSOM (CSS Object Model) is an object representation of the CSS rules that the browser creates when a page loads. It works in the same way as the DOM but for styles: every CSSOM node contains information about selectors, properties and values. The browser builds the CSSOM from all the CSS files, the inline styles and the style attributes.\\nHow does the CSSOM interact with the DOM?\\nOnce the browser has built the DOM (the tree of HTML elements) and the CSSOM (the tree of styles), it combines them into the render tree. The render tree contains only the visible elements and their computed styles. An element with \`display: none\`, for example, does not get into the render tree, although it remains in the DOM. The render tree is then used for the layout stage (calculating the geometry) and paint (drawing the pixels).\\nA code example\\n\\nIn this example the browser first parses the HTML and builds the DOM (the node div#app). Then it loads and parses the CSS, creating the CSSOM (the rule for #app). After that the render tree is formed: div#app with the colour blue and a size of 16px. If the CSS were blocked (by a slow server, for example), the rendering would be delayed until the CSSOM had been built fully.\\nConclusion\\nThe CSSOM is critically important for pages to display correctly: it determines every element's styles. Understanding its interaction with the DOM helps you optimize the critical rendering path — by minimizing blocking CSS resources to speed loading up, for example.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <div id=\\"app\\">Hello</div>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\\n/* styles.css */\\n#app { color: blue; font-size: 16px; }"],[150,"The layout is recalculated (a reflow) when elements' geometric properties change — width, height, position, spacing — or when elements are added to or removed from the DOM. A reflow is also triggered when the window is resized, when CSS pseudo-classes are activated (:hover, for example), when certain properties are read (offsetHeight, scrollTop) and when the content changes (text, for example). It is an expensive operation, so people try to minimize it.","When does the layout get recalculated (a reflow)?\\nRecalculating the layout (also called a reflow) is the rendering stage at which the browser computes the geometry (the dimensions and positions) of every element on the page. It happens every time a property that affects an element's size or position changes.\\nThe main causes of a reflow:\\n\\n- A change to geometric CSS properties: width, height, margin, padding, border, top, left, display (switching to block/inline-block), position.\\n- Adding or removing elements from the DOM.\\n- A change to an element's content (replacing text or an image, for example).\\n- The activation of CSS pseudo-classes such as :hover and :focus.\\n- A change to the browser window's dimensions (resize).\\n- Reading certain properties that force the browser to perform a reflow: offsetHeight, offsetWidth, scrollTop, scrollHeight, clientTop, getComputedStyle() and others.\\nExample code that causes a reflow:\\n\\nHow do you minimize reflows?\\n\\n- Group style changes together (use classList.add or cssText).\\n- Work with elements outside the flow (position: absolute/fixed).\\n- Use a documentFragment for adding elements in bulk.\\n- Avoid reading properties that cause a reflow inside loops.\\nConclusion: understanding reflow matters for optimizing the performance of web pages, especially with animations and the dynamic updating of content. Minimizing layout recalculations speeds rendering up and improves the user experience.","const el = document.getElementById('box');\\nel.style.width = '200px'; // a change of geometry -> a reflow\\nconst height = el.offsetHeight; // a forced read -> a reflow\\nel.style.height = '300px'; // one more reflow"],[151,"Rendering is blocked by \`<script>\` without attributes and by \`<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\">\`. The browser stops building the DOM until it has loaded and executed the script or applied the styles. In React applications this topic matters when configuring \`index.html\`, SSR/SSG and lazy loading.","What \\"blocking rendering\\" means\\nThe browser builds the DOM in sequence, from the top down. If it meets a tag that requires a resource to be loaded or executed, it stops and waits. The page is not displayed until that resource has been processed.\\n\`<script>\` — it blocks the parsing of the DOM\\n\\nThe difference between \`async\` and \`defer\`:\\n\\n- \`async\` — it runs at the moment it loads; the order is not guaranteed\\n\\n- \`defer\` — it runs after parsing, in the order of declaration\\n\\nReact applications (CRA, Vite) add \`defer\` to the bundle by default.\\n\`<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\">\` — it blocks rendering\\n\\nCSS blocks rendering because the browser has to build the CSSOM before painting — otherwise the page would \\"flash\\" without styles (FOUC).\\nWhat does NOT block rendering\\n\\nHow this looks in practice\\n\\nReact specifics\\nIn React you rarely work with these tags directly, but the topic comes up in several contexts:","<!-- ❌ Blocks: the browser will stop parsing until it has loaded and executed this -->\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- ✅ async: loads in parallel, runs as soon as it has loaded -->\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" async><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- ✅ defer: loads in parallel, runs after the DOM has been parsed fully -->\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- ❌ Blocks: the browser will not draw a single pixel without the CSS -->\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n\\n<!-- ✅ Loading non-critical CSS without blocking -->\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"print.css\\" media=\\"print\\">\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"heavy.css\\" media=\\"(min-width: 1200px)\\">\\n\\n<!-- Images — they do not block, they load in parallel -->\\n<img src=\\"photo.jpg\\">\\n\\n<!-- Fonts through font-face — they do not block parsing but can cause FOIT -->\\n<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"font.woff2\\" as=\\"font\\" crossorigin>\\n\\n<!-- preload/prefetch — merely hints to the browser, they do not block -->\\n<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"chunk.js\\" as=\\"script\\">\\n<link rel=\\"prefetch\\" href=\\"next-page.js\\">\\n\\n<head>\\n  <!-- ❌ Blocking CSS — fine for critical styles -->\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"critical.css\\">\\n\\n  <!-- ✅ Non-critical CSS — loaded asynchronously with the onload trick -->\\n  <link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"non-critical.css\\" as=\\"style\\"\\n        onload=\\"this.rel='stylesheet'\\">\\n</head>\\n\\n<body>\\n  <!-- the content -->\\n\\n  <!-- ✅ Scripts at the end of body or with defer — the DOM is already built -->\\n  <script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>\\n</body>\\n\\n// Code splitting — Webpack/Vite will add defer automatically\\nconst LazyPage = React.lazy(() => import('./Page'));\\n\\n// Next.js — built-in management of critical CSS\\n// Components' styles are inlined into <head>, the rest is deferred\\n\\n// Vite/CRA index.html — the bundle always comes with defer\\n// <script type=\\"module\\" src=\\"/src/main.jsx\\"><\/script>\\n// type=\\"module\\" behaves like defer by default"],[152,"Time to Interactive (TTI) is a performance metric showing how many seconds after loading begins the page becomes fully interactive. It takes into account the time at which the content is displayed, the main scripts have run and the user can interact with the elements without delay. TTI helps you assess how quickly a user can start working with a page.","What is Time to Interactive (TTI)?\\nTime to Interactive (TTI) is a web page performance metric that measures the time from the start of navigation to the moment the page becomes fully interactive. Full interactivity means the content is displayed, the main scripts have been loaded and executed, and the user can interact with the elements (pressing buttons or filling in forms, for example) without any noticeable delay.\\nHow is TTI measured?\\nTTI is computed from two key moments:\\n\\n- The page has to be displayed visually (First Contentful Paint).\\n- The browser's main thread has to be free of long tasks for at least 5 seconds.\\nLong tasks are tasks that run for more than 50 ms and block interaction. TTI is recorded once the network and the main thread have stabilized.\\nExample code for tracking TTI\\nAlthough TTI is normally measured with tools such as Lighthouse, you can estimate it approximately with a Performance Observer:\\n\\nWhere is TTI applied?\\nTTI is critically important for the user experience, especially on mobile devices and over slow connections. A low TTI means the user can start interacting quickly, which improves engagement and conversion. The metric is used in performance audits (Lighthouse, WebPageTest) and for optimizing the loading of scripts, reducing the size of JavaScript and eliminating long tasks.\\nConclusion\\nTTI helps developers assess how quickly a page becomes ready for interaction. Apply this metric to optimize the critical rendering path, especially on sites with a lot of JavaScript, in order to ensure a smooth user experience.","const observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.name === 'first-contentful-paint') {\\n      console.log('FCP:', entry.startTime);\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'paint', buffered: true });\\n\\n// TTI requires more complex logic, usually through libraries"],[153,"The browser starts with a DNS request to get the server's IP address, then establishes a TCP connection and sends an HTTP request. Once it has received the HTML document, the parser builds the DOM tree, while the CSS and JavaScript load in parallel. The CSS forms the CSSOM, then the browser creates the render tree, computes the layout (the elements' geometry) and performs the paint (drawing the pixels).","The overall rendering process\\nWhen the user types a URL in the address bar, the browser begins a sequence of steps known as the critical rendering path. This process involves several stages, from resolving DNS to drawing pixels on the screen.\\nThe main stages\\n\\n- The DNS request: the browser turns the domain name into the server's IP address.\\n- The TCP connection: a connection to the server is established (normally over HTTPS).\\n- The HTTP request: the browser sends a GET request to the server for the HTML document.\\n- Parsing the HTML: the browser starts taking the HTML apart and building the DOM tree. On discovering external resources (CSS, JS) it initiates their loading.\\n- Building the CSSOM: the CSS rules are turned into the CSS Object Model.\\n- The render tree: combining the DOM and the CSSOM into a tree that contains only the visible elements.\\n- Layout: computing the exact dimensions and positions of every element.\\n- Paint: drawing the pixels on the screen.\\nAn example with code\\nConsider a simple HTML document:\\n\\nThe browser first loads the HTML, then finds the \`<link>\` and starts loading the CSS. The parsing of the HTML carries on, but rendering is blocked until the CSS has loaded fully. Once the CSS has arrived, the CSSOM is built, and then the render tree. Layout and paint follow. The JavaScript at the end is loaded and executed, which may change the DOM or the CSSOM and cause a recalculation.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the critical rendering path helps you optimize the performance of web pages — by minimizing blocking resources and using asynchronous loading of scripts, for example.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\">\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <div>Hello World</div>\\n  <script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[154,"The main metrics for a page's loading performance include First Contentful Paint (FCP), Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), First Input Delay (FID), Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) and Time to Interactive (TTI). FCP measures the time until content is first displayed, LCP the loading time of the largest element, FID the delay on the first interaction, CLS the visual stability, and TTI the time until full interactivity. These metrics help you assess the user experience and optimize performance.","Metrics for a page's loading performance\\nA web page's loading performance is critically important for the user experience and for ranking in search engines. Standardized metrics are used to assess it, measuring various aspects of loading, from the first display of content to the stability of the layout. The main metrics are part of the Web Vitals set recommended by Google.\\nThe main metrics\\n\\n- First Contentful Paint (FCP) — the time at which the browser first displays any content (text, an image, a canvas). A good value is less than 1.8 seconds.\\n- Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) — the loading time of the largest visible element (an image, a video, a block of text). The target is less than 2.5 seconds.\\n- First Input Delay (FID) — the delay between the user's first interaction (a click, a key press) and the browser's response. Good is less than 100 ms.\\n- Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) — a measure of visual stability that assesses unexpected shifts in the layout. A good value is less than 0.1.\\n- Time to Interactive (TTI) — the time at which the page becomes fully interactive (ready for interaction). The target is less than 3.8 seconds.\\nAn example of measuring with the Performance API\\n\\nUse\\nThese metrics are used to monitor the real user experience (RUM) and for lab testing (Lighthouse). Optimizing for them involves compressing images, minifying JavaScript, using caching and preloading critical resources.\\nConclusion: metrics for a page's loading performance are necessary for assessing a site's speed and stability objectively. Measuring and optimizing them regularly helps improve the user experience and raise conversion and SEO positions.","// Getting the metrics through a PerformanceObserver\\nconst observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    console.log(\`\${entry.name}: \${entry.startTime} ms\`);\\n  });\\n});\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'largest-contentful-paint', buffered: true });\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'first-input', buffered: true });\\nobserver.observe({ type: 'layout-shift', buffered: true });"],[155,"The browser loads the HTML and starts parsing it, building the DOM. When it meets CSS it builds the CSSOM, while JavaScript blocks parsing until it has run. The DOM and CSSOM are then combined into the render tree, after which layout (computing the geometry) and paint (drawing the pixels) happen.","The stages of a page's loading\\nWhen the browser receives an HTML document, it goes through several key stages in order to display the page. This process is called the critical rendering path. Understanding these stages helps developers optimize loading speed.\\n1. Parsing the HTML and building the DOM\\nThe browser starts parsing the HTML from the first byte. It turns the markup into the Document Object Model (DOM) — a tree of nodes in which every tag becomes an element. The parsing proceeds in sequence but can be paused.\\n2. Loading and parsing the CSS (the CSSOM)\\nWhen the browser meets a \`<link>\` or \`<style>\` tag, it starts loading the CSS. Parsing the CSS does not block the building of the DOM, but it does block rendering — the browser will not show the page until it has built the CSSOM (the CSS object model). The CSSOM is a tree of rules that are applied to elements.\\n3. The effect of JavaScript\\nJavaScript can block the parsing of the HTML. If a script is met without the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes, the browser stops building the DOM, loads and executes the script, and only then carries on. This can slow loading down. The \`defer\` attribute postpones execution until parsing has finished, while \`async\` runs the script asynchronously without blocking the parsing.\\n4. Building the render tree\\nOnce the DOM and the CSSOM are ready, the browser combines them into the render tree. Only the visible elements get into it (\`<head>\` or elements with \`display: none\` are excluded, for example).\\n5. Layout (reflow)\\nAt this stage the browser computes the exact dimensions and positions of every element on the page. It is a resource-intensive process, especially with complex layouts.\\n6. Paint\\nThe final stage is turning the render tree into pixels on the screen. The browser draws the layers and applies colours, shadows and textures.\\nExample code demonstrating the blocking\\n\\nIn this example the browser will first load the CSS, then stop at the script, execute it, and only then carry on parsing and rendering.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the stages of loading helps developers optimize performance: put the CSS in \`<head>\` and the JavaScript at the end of \`<body>\` with the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes, so as not to block rendering and to improve the user experience.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"> <!-- blocks rendering -->\\n  <script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script> <!-- blocks the parsing of the HTML -->\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <p>The text will appear once the script has loaded</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[156,"CSS variables (custom properties) work at the browser level, support inheritance and can be changed dynamically through JavaScript or media queries. SCSS variables are compiled into static values at build time and cannot be changed at runtime. CSS variables are available in the DOM and suit theming, while SCSS variables suit the convenience of development and the reuse of values.","The main difference: when they are computed\\nCSS variables (custom properties) are computed by the browser while the page is running, whereas SCSS variables are computed at the preprocessor's compilation stage. This key difference determines all the other characteristics.\\nDynamism and inheritance\\nCSS variables are inherited through the DOM and can be overridden in any selector, including media queries and pseudo-classes. SCSS variables have lexical scope (blocks of code) and do not respond to changes in the DOM.\\n\\nUse in JavaScript\\nCSS variables can be read and changed through JS, which is convenient for dynamic themes or animations. SCSS variables are unavailable at runtime.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse CSS variables for dynamic styles, theming and responsiveness. SCSS variables suit static values that do not change at runtime, and improving the readability of code at the development stage.","/* SCSS */\\n$primary: blue;\\n.button { color: $primary; }\\n.dark .button { $primary: white; /* Error: this will not override it */ }\\n\\n/* CSS */\\n:root { --primary: blue; }\\n.button { color: var(--primary); }\\n.dark .button { --primary: white; /* Works */ }\\n\\n// Reading a CSS variable\\ngetComputedStyle(element).getPropertyValue('--primary');\\n// Changing it\\nelement.style.setProperty('--primary', 'red');"],[157,"Yes, you can do without SCSS in modern projects. Modern CSS supports variables (custom properties), nesting, calc() and other features that used to be available only through preprocessors. SCSS is still useful for mixins, loops and the advanced organization of code, however. The decision depends on the team and on the project's requirements.","Can you do without SCSS in modern projects?\\nYes, in most modern projects you can do without SCSS, since native CSS has evolved considerably. Many capabilities that used to be available only through preprocessors are now implemented in CSS itself. This does not mean SCSS is entirely obsolete, however — it still provides convenient tools for complex projects.\\nWhat does modern CSS offer?\\n\\n- Variables (custom properties): \`--primary-color: #333;\` with support for being changed dynamically through JavaScript.\\n- Nesting: native support for nested selectors (Chrome 120+, Safari 17.2+).\\n- Functions: \`calc()\`, \`min()\`, \`max()\`, \`clamp()\` for flexible calculations.\\n- Colour functions: \`color-mix()\`, \`light-dark()\`.\\n- CSS modules: \`@import\` with support for modules, and \`@layer\` for managing the cascade.\\nA comparison\\nSCSS:\\n\\nModern CSS:\\n\\nWhen is SCSS still useful?\\n\\n- Mixins and functions: for reusing complex blocks of styles.\\n- Loops and conditionals: for generating a great many classes (a grid, for example).\\n- Managing colours: the \`darken()\` and \`lighten()\` functions are more convenient than \`color-mix()\`.\\n- Old projects: migration may be expensive and unjustified.\\nConclusion\\nDoing without SCSS is justified in new projects with modern browsers, where performance and reducing dependencies matter. For complex systems with a lot of repeated code, however, or when support for old browsers is necessary, SCSS remains a useful tool. The decision should be based on the project's requirements and on the team's experience.","$primary: #3498db;\\n.button {\\n  background: $primary;\\n  &:hover {\\n    background: darken($primary, 10%);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n:root {\\n  --primary: #3498db;\\n}\\n.button {\\n  background: var(--primary);\\n}\\n.button:hover {\\n  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--primary), black 10%);\\n}"],[158,"A module's public API is the set of functions, classes or variables the module exports explicitly for other parts of the application to use. Everything that is not exported is considered private and unavailable from outside. This lets you hide the internal implementation and provide only the interface that is needed. In JavaScript, for example, export is used to declare the public API.","What is a module's public API?\\nA module's public API is the contract the module provides to external code. It determines which functions, classes, constants or types are available to be imported and used in other modules. The internal details of the implementation, such as helper functions or private variables, stay hidden. This is a key principle of encapsulation that helps keep code clean, reduce coupling and make refactoring easier.\\nHow does it work in JavaScript?\\nIn modern JavaScript (ES6+) a module's public API is defined with the \`export\` keyword. You can export individual entities or use \`export default\` for the main export. Everything that is not exported is private and unavailable from outside.\\n\\nIn another file you can import only what you need:\\n\\nWhy is this needed?\\nA module's public API lets you:\\n\\n- Hide internal complexity and implementation details.\\n- Reduce the risk of internal functions being used by accident.\\n- Make testing and the replacement of modules easier.\\n- Create clear boundaries between the parts of an application.\\nThis matters especially in large projects, where modules are developed by different teams or reused in several places.\\nConclusion\\nA module's public API is the foundation of a modular architecture on the frontend. It helps you organize code and make it more predictable and easier to maintain. Use explicit exports in order to control exactly what is available from outside, and avoid exporting everything indiscriminately.","// math.js — a module with a public API\\n\\nexport function add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nexport const PI = 3.14159;\\n\\n// A private function, not exported\\nfunction helper() {\\n  console.log('Internal helper');\\n}\\n\\n// app.js\\nimport { add, PI } from './math.js';\\n\\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // 5\\nconsole.log(PI); // 3.14159"],[159,"SEO (Search Engine Optimization) depends directly on HTML, since search engines analyse a page's structure and content. The proper use of semantic tags (\`<header>\`, \`<article>\`, \`<footer>\`, for example) helps crawlers understand the hierarchy of the content. Meta tags such as \`<title>\` and \`<meta name=\\"description\\">\` affect how the page appears in search results. Clean, valid HTML improves a site's indexing and ranking.","The connection between SEO and HTML\\nSEO (Search Engine Optimization) and HTML are inseparably connected, since HTML is the basis of every web page. Search engines such as Google use crawlers to scan HTML code in order to understand a page's content, its structure and its relevance to search queries. Good HTML directly affects how search crawlers interpret and rank a site.\\nThe key HTML elements for SEO\\n\\n- Semantic tags: using the tags \`<header>\`, \`<nav>\`, \`<main>\`, \`<article>\`, \`<section>\` and \`<footer>\` helps search engines understand the document's logical structure. This improves indexing and can increase the chances of appearing in rich snippets.\\n- Meta tags: the \`<title>\` tag sets the page's title, which is shown in search results. The \`<meta name=\\"description\\">\` meta tag provides a brief description that may also be shown in the snippet. Filling these tags in properly with keywords increases the click-through rate (CTR).\\n- Headings (h1-h6): the \`<h1>\` tag should be unique to every page and contain the main topic. The hierarchy of headings (h1, h2, h3) helps search crawlers assess the importance of the content.\\n- The alt attribute on images: the \`alt\` attribute on an \`<img>\` tag describes the image's content. This not only improves accessibility for users with disabilities but also lets search engines index the images, which can attract additional traffic.\\n- Clean, valid code: the absence of errors in the HTML (unclosed tags, for example) makes the page easier to scan. Using a proper structure and avoiding duplicated content also has a positive effect on SEO.\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion: SEO and HTML work in tandem: competent semantic markup and the proper use of meta tags make a site more comprehensible to search engines, which improves its visibility and ranking. Applying these principles is mandatory for any site aiming at organic traffic.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html lang=\\"en\\">\\n<head>\\n    <meta charset=\\"UTF-8\\">\\n    <title>How to bake the perfect bread | Recipes</title>\\n    <meta name=\\"description\\" content=\\"A step-by-step recipe for home-made bread with a crisp crust. Ingredients, baking time and the secrets of the dough.\\">\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n    <header>\\n        <nav><!-- The navigation --></nav>\\n    </header>\\n    <main>\\n        <article>\\n            <h1>The perfect home-made bread</h1>\\n            <section>\\n                <h2>Ingredients</h2>\\n                <!-- The list of ingredients -->\\n            </section>\\n            <section>\\n                <h2>The step-by-step process</h2>\\n                <img src=\\"dough.jpg\\" alt=\\"Kneaded bread dough\\">\\n            </section>\\n        </article>\\n    </main>\\n    <footer><!-- The footer --></footer>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[160,"Not all scripts block the parsing of HTML. Ordinary scripts without attributes block parsing until they have been loaded and executed fully. Scripts with the async or defer attributes do not block parsing, but they behave differently: async loads in parallel and runs as soon as it has loaded, while defer runs after parsing has finished. This lets you speed a page's loading up.","How scripts affect the parsing of HTML\\nWhen the browser meets a \`<script>\` tag without attributes, it stops parsing the HTML, loads the script, executes it, and only then carries on taking the document apart. This can slow the display of the page down, especially if the script is heavy or loads from a slow server.\\nThe async and defer attributes\\nThe \`async\` attribute lets a script be loaded in parallel with the parsing of the HTML. As soon as the script has loaded it runs, pausing the parsing. The \`defer\` attribute also loads the script in parallel but postpones its execution until the document has finished being parsed. Neither attribute blocks parsing, but \`defer\` guarantees the order in which scripts run, while \`async\` does not.\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`defer\` for scripts that have to run after the DOM has loaded fully, and \`async\` for independent scripts such as analytics. This improves performance and the user experience.","<!-- A blocking script -->\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- Non-blocking async -->\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\" async><\/script>\\n\\n<!-- Non-blocking defer -->\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[161,"Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) is a metric that measures how often and how far the elements on a page shift unexpectedly while it loads. A high CLS degrades the user experience, since the user may click the wrong button by accident or lose their place in the text. CLS is computed as the sum of all the unexpected shifts of visible elements multiplied by their share of the viewport.","What is Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)?\\nCumulative Layout Shift (CLS) is one of the three main Web Vitals metrics, and it assesses a web page's visual stability. It measures how often and by how much interface elements shift unexpectedly while the page is loading. A low CLS means the user sees stable content, while a high one means the elements \\"jump\\", which can cause irritation and interaction errors.\\nHow is CLS calculated?\\nCLS is computed as the sum of all the unexpected shifts of visible elements. For every shift a score = impact fraction * distance fraction is calculated. The impact fraction is the share of the viewport affected by the shift, and the distance fraction is the distance the element moved relative to the viewport. The final CLS is the sum of all such scores over the page's lifetime.\\nExamples and the causes of a high CLS\\nThe main causes of a high CLS:\\n\\n- Images and video without explicit dimensions (width/height).\\n- Dynamically loaded fonts that change the size of the text.\\n- Advertising blocks inserted after the main content has loaded.\\n- Animations that change the position of elements.\\nA practical example\\nConsider HTML code that causes a shift:\\n\\nIf the image has no dimensions set, the browser will display the text first and then, once the image has loaded, the text will shift downwards. To avoid this, the dimensions have to be stated explicitly:\\n\\nOr you can use CSS containers with a fixed aspect ratio.\\nConclusion\\nCLS is an important metric for ensuring a comfortable user experience. It should be optimized, especially on pages with dynamic content, images and advertising. It is recommended always to set the dimensions of media elements and to avoid inserting content after the main layout has loaded.","<div>\\n  <img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"Example\\">\\n  <p>Some text</p>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div>\\n  <img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"Example\\" width=\\"400\\" height=\\"300\\">\\n  <p>Some text</p>\\n</div>"],[162,"First Contentful Paint (FCP) is a performance metric that measures the time from the start of a page's loading to the moment the browser displays the first piece of content — text or an image, for example. It helps you assess how quickly the user sees that the page is starting to load. A good FCP value is less than 1.8 seconds.","What is First Contentful Paint (FCP)?\\nFirst Contentful Paint (FCP) is one of the key performance metrics for web pages and is part of the Core Web Vitals. It measures the time in milliseconds from the moment the user starts navigating to the page (typing a URL or clicking a link, for example) to the moment the browser first paints any text or graphical content. That may be text, an image, an SVG, a canvas or even a non-empty \`<div>\` element. FCP does not take background images or fonts loaded through \`@font-face\` into account.\\nHow is FCP measured?\\nFCP is recorded by the browser at the moment the first content is painted in the viewport. Tools such as Lighthouse, PageSpeed Insights or the Performance API in the browser are used to measure it. An example of getting FCP through JavaScript:\\n\\nWhy does FCP matter?\\nFCP directly affects the user's perception of loading speed. If the FCP is high (more than 2.5 seconds), the user may think the page is not working and leave. A low FCP (less than 1.8 seconds) creates a sense of fast loading. The metric is especially critical for content sites, online shops and news portals, where the first impression decides a great deal.\\nHow do you improve FCP?\\n\\n- Minimize render-blocking CSS and JavaScript.\\n- Use critical CSS (inline styles for the first screen).\\n- Optimize images (compression, the modern WebP format).\\n- Set up resource caching through a CDN.\\n- Remove unused code and libraries.\\nConclusion\\nFCP is the basic metric for assessing a page's initial loading. Improving it directly raises the user experience and can have a positive effect on SEO and conversion. It is recommended to monitor FCP in real conditions regularly and to optimize the critical rendering paths.","new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\\n  const entries = list.getEntries();\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    console.log('FCP:', entry.startTime, 'ms');\\n  });\\n}).observe({ type: 'paint', buffered: true });"],[163,"Yes, reaching the DOM through JavaScript can cause a reflow. A reflow is the process of recalculating the geometry of a page's elements. When you read or change properties that affect elements' dimensions or positions (offsetHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop, for example), the browser may perform a forced reflow in order to return up-to-date values. This can have a negative effect on performance, especially with frequent operations.","What a reflow is and how it relates to the DOM\\nA reflow (or layout) is the rendering stage at which the browser computes the dimensions and positions of every element. It happens when a page first loads, and also on any change that may affect the geometry: resizing the window, adding or removing elements, changing styles (width, height, margin, padding and so on).\\nJavaScript can both read and change the DOM. Some read operations, such as getting offsetHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop or getComputedStyle, can force the browser to perform a reflow in order to return up-to-date values. This is called a forced synchronous reflow.\\nExamples of operations that cause a reflow\\n\\n- Reading properties: offsetHeight, offsetWidth, clientHeight, clientWidth, scrollTop, scrollLeft, getBoundingClientRect, getComputedStyle.\\n- Changing styles that affect the geometry: width, height, margin, padding, display, position, float and so on.\\n- Adding or removing elements from the DOM.\\n- Changing the content (textContent, for example), if it affects the dimensions.\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example every access to offsetHeight and every change to width may cause a reflow. To minimize the number of reflows it is recommended to group the read and write operations together, or to use techniques such as reading all the values before making any changes.\\nHow to avoid unnecessary reflows\\n\\n- Use classes to change styles rather than changing properties directly.\\n- Work with elements outside the flow (with display: none, for example — then change them and show them afterwards).\\n- Use a documentFragment for adding elements in bulk.\\n- Cache the values that may cause a reflow if they do not change.\\nConclusion: understanding reflow matters for optimizing the performance of web pages. Avoid frequent forced reflows, especially in animations or when working with large lists. Group your operations together and use modern approaches such as requestAnimationFrame to synchronize changes with the browser's rendering cycle.","const el = document.getElementById('myElement');\\n// Reading offsetHeight causes a reflow\\nconst height = el.offsetHeight;\\n// Changing the width also causes a reflow\\nel.style.width = '200px';\\n// If you read after the change, there will be another reflow\\nconst newHeight = el.offsetHeight;"],[164,"When the browser meets CSS (external or internal), it carries on parsing the HTML but pauses rendering until the CSS has loaded fully and the CSSOM has been built. On meeting ordinary JavaScript (without async/defer), the browser stops parsing the HTML entirely, loads and executes the script, and only then carries on. This is done because the script may change the DOM or the CSSOM.","The effect of CSS and JavaScript on the parsing of HTML\\nThe browser builds the DOM (Document Object Model) from the HTML and the CSSOM (CSS Object Model) from the CSS. These two trees are combined into the render tree in order to paint the page. CSS and JavaScript affect this process differently.\\nCSS (render blocking)\\nWhen the HTML parser meets a \`<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\">\` or \`<style>\` tag, it does not stop parsing the HTML but it does block rendering. The browser will not display the page until the CSSOM has been built fully. This is necessary in order to avoid FOUC (a Flash of Unstyled Content).\\nJavaScript (parser blocking)\\nWhen the parser meets an ordinary \`<script>\` tag (without the \`async\` or \`defer\` attributes), it stops parsing the HTML entirely. The browser loads the script (if it is external), executes it, and only then carries on parsing. This is because the script may change the DOM (through \`document.write\`) or reach the CSSOM.\\nAn example\\n\\nIn this example the browser will first load the CSS, then stop at the script, load and execute it, and only then carry on parsing and painting.\\nOptimization\\n\\n- For CSS: use \`media\` attributes to indicate that the styles are not critical to the initial rendering.\\n- For JavaScript: use \`async\` (the script runs as soon as it has loaded, without blocking the parsing) or \`defer\` (the script runs once the parsing of the HTML has finished).\\nConclusion: understanding render blocking and parser blocking is critically important for optimizing a page's loading speed (the Core Web Vitals). Use \`async\`/\`defer\` for scripts and minimize the critical CSS for a fast first display.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"> <!-- Render blocking -->\\n  <script src=\\"script.js\\"><\/script> <!-- Parser blocking -->\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <p>Hello</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[165,"The getItem method returns null if the key does not exist in localStorage. If you do not check that value, the code may fail with an error when you try to call methods on null — JSON.parse(null), for example. Always check the result of getItem for null before using it.","Why null matters when working with localStorage\\nThe \`localStorage.getItem(key)\` method returns \`null\` if the given key is absent from storage. This is standard behaviour that has to be allowed for in order to avoid runtime errors.\\nAn example of the problem\\nConsider some typical code without a check:\\n\\nIf the key 'user' is absent, \`getItem\` will return \`null\`, and \`JSON.parse(null)\` will throw an exception.\\nThe correct approach\\nAlways check the result before using it:\\n\\nYou can also use the nullish coalescing operator (\`??\`) to supply a default value:\\n\\nConclusion\\nChecking for null when getting data out of localStorage is mandatory for an application to work reliably, especially on the first run or after storage has been cleared.","const data = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\\nconsole.log(data.name); // An error if the key is absent\\n\\nconst raw = localStorage.getItem('user');\\nif (raw === null) {\\n  console.log('The data was not found');\\n} else {\\n  const data = JSON.parse(raw);\\n  console.log(data.name);\\n}\\n\\nconst data = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user') ?? '{}');"],[166,"localStorage provides methods for storing data in the browser. The main methods are: setItem(key, value) for saving, getItem(key) for retrieving, removeItem(key) for deleting a single entry, clear() for clearing the whole store, plus key(index) for access by index and the length property for getting the number of entries.","The main methods of localStorage\\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API and lets you store data in the browser with no expiry. The data is preserved even after the tab or the browser has been closed. All the methods are synchronous and work only with strings.\\nThe methods and their use\\n\\n- setItem(key, value) — saves a value under a key. If the key already exists, the value is overwritten.\\n- getItem(key) — returns the value for a key, or null if the key is not found.\\n- removeItem(key) — deletes the entry for a key.\\n- clear() — deletes every entry from localStorage.\\n- key(index) — returns the name of the key at an index (starting from 0).\\n- length — a property showing the number of items stored.\\nA code example\\n\\nImportant characteristics\\n\\n- Every value is converted to a string automatically. For objects, use JSON.stringify() and JSON.parse().\\n- The store is limited in size (usually 5-10 MB per domain).\\n- The data is available only within a single protocol and domain.\\nConclusion: localStorage is convenient for storing user settings, tokens or cached data that has to survive between sessions. You should not store confidential information in it, however, since the data is reachable through JavaScript.","// We save the data\\nlocalStorage.setItem('username', 'John');\\nlocalStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark');\\n\\n// We get the data\\nconst user = localStorage.getItem('username'); // 'John'\\n\\n// We delete a single entry\\nlocalStorage.removeItem('theme');\\n\\n// We get the number of entries\\nconsole.log(localStorage.length); // 1\\n\\n// Access by index\\nconst firstKey = localStorage.key(0); // 'username'\\n\\n// We clear the whole store\\nlocalStorage.clear();"],[167,"localStorage lets you store data in the browser with no expiry. To retrieve data, the getItem() method is used, which takes a key and returns a string. If the key is not found, null is returned. The data is stored as strings, so objects have to be serialized with JSON.stringify() and parsed with JSON.parse().","What localStorage is and how to work with it\\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API and provides the ability to store data in the user's browser. The data is preserved between sessions and has no expiry until it is deleted by hand or through JavaScript. Unlike cookies, localStorage is not sent to the server with every HTTP request, which makes it efficient for storing settings, tokens or cached data.\\nThe getItem() method\\nTo get data out of localStorage the \`getItem(key)\` method is used, where key is a string corresponding to the key the data was saved under. The method returns a string, or null if the key is absent. Since localStorage stores only strings, JSON.parse() has to be used in order to work with objects or arrays.\\n\\nPractical use\\nlocalStorage is often used to store user settings (a theme, a language), the state of the interface (expanded menus) or temporary data that does not require server-side processing. For example, you can save the filter a user has selected on a page so that it is restored on reload.\\nConclusion\\nlocalStorage is a simple, fast way of storing data on the client, suitable for small volumes of information (up to 5-10 MB). Use it to cache settings or state, but avoid storing sensitive data, since it is reachable through JavaScript.","// Saving an object\\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\\n\\n// Retrieving and parsing it\\nconst storedUser = localStorage.getItem('user');\\nif (storedUser) {\\n  const parsedUser = JSON.parse(storedUser);\\n  console.log(parsedUser.name); // Alice\\n} else {\\n  console.log('The key was not found');\\n}"],[168,"localStorage can store only strings. If you try to save an object, an array or a number, they will be converted to a string automatically through the toString() method, which leads to data being lost or to an incorrect format. Data therefore has to be serialized to JSON with JSON.stringify() before saving, and deserialized with JSON.parse() when read.","Why does localStorage require serialization?\\nlocalStorage is a mechanism for storing data in the browser that works exclusively with strings. When you try to save a value that is not a string (an object, an array, a number or a boolean, for example), the browser calls the \`toString()\` method on that value automatically. For objects this results in the string \`[object Object]\` being stored, which makes the data useless.\\nHow do you work with data properly?\\nTo save and restore complex data structures correctly, JSON serialization has to be used. The \`JSON.stringify()\` method turns an object or array into a JSON string, and \`JSON.parse()\` restores the original structure.\\n\\nExamples of the problems without serialization\\n\\n- Saving a number: \`localStorage.setItem('count', 42)\` — the string '42' will be stored, but when read it will be a string rather than a number.\\n- Saving an array: \`localStorage.setItem('arr', [1,2,3])\` — the string '1,2,3' will be stored, which is not valid JSON.\\n- Saving an object: \`localStorage.setItem('obj', {a:1})\` — the string '[object Object]' will be stored.\\nConclusion\\nSerialization through JSON is a mandatory step when working with localStorage for anything other than plain strings. It guarantees the integrity and the correct type of the data when it is used later.","// Saving an object\\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\\n\\n// Reading and restoring it\\nconst storedUser = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\\nconsole.log(storedUser.name); // 'Alice'"],[169,"The main errors when working with localStorage include exceeding the storage limit (usually 5-10 MB), which raises a QuotaExceededError exception. Security problems can also arise, such as XSS attacks, since the data is stored in plain form. In addition, localStorage does not support automatic synchronization between tabs, and the data may be lost when the browser's cache is cleared.","The main errors when working with localStorage\\nlocalStorage is a mechanism for storing data on the client side in the browser. Despite its simplicity it has a number of limitations and potential problems that are important to bear in mind during development.\\nExceeding the storage limit\\nMost browsers set a limit on the size of the data in localStorage (usually 5-10 MB per domain). When you try to save data beyond that limit, a \`QuotaExceededError\` exception is raised. This is especially relevant for applications that store large volumes of data, such as a cache of images or logs.\\n\\nSecurity problems\\nThe data in localStorage is stored in plain form and is reachable by any JavaScript code on the page. This makes it vulnerable to XSS attacks. An attacker can gain access to sensitive information such as authentication tokens or user settings. Never store passwords, API keys or other confidential data in localStorage.\\nNo synchronization between tabs\\nChanges to localStorage in one tab are not reflected automatically in other tabs of the same domain. To synchronize them you have to use the \`storage\` event, which fires when the data changes in another tab.\\n\\nData loss when the cache is cleared\\nThe user can clear the browser's data, localStorage included, at any moment. This will cause every piece of saved data to be lost. localStorage is therefore not suitable for storing critically important information that has to be preserved permanently.\\nConclusion\\nlocalStorage is convenient for storing small volumes of non-critical data, such as interface settings or temporary state. For storing sensitive information, or data that requires synchronization, however, it is better to use server-side solutions or safer mechanisms such as sessionStorage or IndexedDB.","try {\\n  localStorage.setItem('key', 'largeData');\\n} catch (e) {\\n  if (e.name === 'QuotaExceededError') {\\n    console.error('The localStorage limit has been exceeded');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nwindow.addEventListener('storage', (event) => {\\n  console.log('The data has changed:', event.key, event.newValue);\\n});"],[170,"localStorage lets you store data in the browser with no expiry. To save, the method setItem('key', 'value') is used. The data is stored as strings, so objects have to be converted with JSON.stringify(). The data is reachable from any window of the same origin.","What localStorage is and how to work with it\\nlocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API and provides the ability to store key-value pairs in the browser. The data is preserved even after the tab or the browser has been closed and has no expiry. This is convenient for storing user settings, the state of the interface or caching small volumes of data.\\nSaving data\\nThe main method for writing is \`setItem(key, value)\`. Both parameters have to be strings. If you need to save an object or an array, use \`JSON.stringify()\` to convert it into a string.\\n\\nReading and deleting\\nTo retrieve data use \`getItem(key)\`, which returns a string or \`null\` if the key is absent. To delete a single item use \`removeItem(key)\`; to clear everything, \`clear()\`.\\n\\nImportant characteristics\\n\\n- The data is stored as strings — any non-string types have to be converted explicitly.\\n- The size of the store is limited (usually 5–10 MB per domain).\\n- localStorage is synchronous — operations can block the main thread, so do not use it for large volumes of data.\\n- The data is available only to the same protocol, domain and port (the same-origin policy).\\nConclusion\\nlocalStorage is a simple, effective way of storing small volumes of data on the client side. Use it to save user settings, the state of the interface or cached data that does not require synchronization with the server. For more complex scenarios (large volumes, working with files), consider IndexedDB or server-side storage.","// We save a string\\nlocalStorage.setItem('username', 'Alice');\\n\\n// We save an object\\nconst user = { name: 'Bob', age: 30 };\\nlocalStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify(user));\\n\\n// We save an array\\nconst colors = ['red', 'green', 'blue'];\\nlocalStorage.setItem('colors', JSON.stringify(colors));\\n\\n// We read a string\\nconst name = localStorage.getItem('username');\\n\\n// We read an object (not forgetting to parse it)\\nconst savedUser = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('user'));\\n\\n// We delete a single item\\nlocalStorage.removeItem('colors');\\n\\n// We clear the whole store\\nlocalStorage.clear();"],[171,"Yes, XML and JSON can be used in various kinds of request, such as GET, POST, PUT and DELETE. JSON is more often applied in REST APIs because of its lightness and simplicity, while XML is used in SOAP and in configuration files. The choice depends on the project's requirements and on compatibility with other systems.","Using XML and JSON in requests\\nXML and JSON are formats for exchanging data that can be used in various kinds of HTTP request. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is more popular in modern web applications thanks to its compactness and its convenience for JavaScript. XML (eXtensible Markup Language) is often applied in enterprise systems and protocols such as SOAP.\\nExamples of use\\nIn a REST API, JSON is normally passed in the body of POST, PUT or PATCH requests, and it can also be part of the response to a GET request. XML can be used in the same way, but it requires additional processing on both the client and the server.\\n\\nConclusion\\nXML and JSON can be used in any kind of request, but JSON is preferable for lightweight, fast applications, while XML suits systems that require a strict structure and extensibility — in SOAP services, for example.","// An example of JSON in a POST request\\nfetch('/api/users', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'John', age: 30 })\\n});\\n\\n// An example of XML in a POST request (using SOAP)\\nconst xml = '<soap:Envelope xmlns:soap=\\"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/\\"><soap:Body>' +\\n  '<GetUser><id>123</id></GetUser></soap:Body></soap:Envelope>';\\nfetch('/api/soap', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/xml' },\\n  body: xml\\n});"],[172,"Figma is used by testers to check a finished interface against the mockups. In Figma you can measure spacing and the dimensions of elements and check colours and fonts. This helps you find discrepancies between the design and the implementation. Figma is also convenient for writing test cases based on the visual components.","Figma's role in testing\\nFigma is a tool for designing interfaces that is used actively by testers to check that markup matches the mockups. Unlike static images, Figma provides access to exact dimensions, spacing, colours and styles, which makes it indispensable for UI testing.\\nHow testers work with Figma\\n\\n- Checking dimensions and spacing: with the \\"Measure\\" tool you can check whether the actual pixel values match the design.\\n- Checking colours: Figma shows HEX, RGB and other colour codes, which lets you be sure the palette is accurate.\\n- Analysing elements' states: the mockups often show hover, active and disabled states that have to be checked in the code.\\n- Writing test cases: from Figma's components you can create checklists for regression testing.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nConclusion\\nFigma helps testers uncover visual defects accurately and quickly, especially at the acceptance-testing stage. Using Figma in the QA process raises the quality of the product and reduces the time spent communicating with designers.","// An example of checking spacing in the code (CSS) and in Figma\\n// Figma: the gap between the button and the input field = 16px\\n// In the code:\\n.button {\\n  margin-bottom: 16px; // it should match\\n}"],[173,"Open the browser's developer tools (F12). Check the console for JavaScript errors. Make sure an event handler has been attached to the button correctly. Check the Network tab in order to see whether requests are being sent to the server. If there are no errors, check whether the script is blocked or the event is being intercepted.","Diagnosing the problem\\nWhen nothing happens after a button is pressed, the first thing to do is open the browser's developer tools (usually the F12 key). This is the main tool for debugging web applications.\\nChecking the console\\nGo to the Console tab. This is where all the JavaScript errors, warnings and logs appear. If there is an error, it normally contains a description and the line number of the code. The error 'Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of null', for example, indicates that you are trying to reach a DOM element that does not exist.\\nChecking the event handler\\nMake sure an event handler has been attached to the button. This can be done through the onclick attribute in the HTML or through addEventListener in JavaScript. An example of attaching one correctly:\\n\\nChecking the network requests\\nIf the event handler is there but nothing happens, go to the Network tab. Press the button and see whether any new requests appear. If there are no requests, the problem is in the client-side code. If there is a request, check its status and the server's response.\\nOther possible causes\\n\\n- The script failed to load because of an error elsewhere in the code\\n- The event is being intercepted by another handler (event.stopPropagation)\\n- The button is inside a form and the form is being submitted instead of the handler being called\\n- A browser extension is blocking the script from running\\nConclusion\\nA systematic approach to debugging — checking the console, the event handlers and the network requests — lets you uncover most problems quickly. Use the developer tools as your main diagnostic instrument.","// HTML: <button id=\\"myButton\\">Click me</button>\\n// JavaScript:\\ndocument.getElementById('myButton').addEventListener('click', function() {\\n  console.log('Button clicked!');\\n  // Your code\\n});"],[174,"A tester needs to understand the structure of an HTML document and be able to find elements by tag, class and ID. It is important to know the basics of CSS: selectors and the properties for working with dimensions, colour and positioning. This helps you describe bugs precisely and check markup.","Why a tester needs HTML and CSS\\nHTML and CSS are the basis of every web interface. A tester who understands these technologies can localize defects more precisely, find elements on a page faster and describe problems competently in a bug tracker. Without this knowledge it is hard to tell a markup error from a logical error in the application.\\nThe main knowledge of HTML\\n\\n- The document's structure: \`<html>\`, \`<head>\`, \`<body>\`.\\n- Semantic tags: \`<header>\`, \`<main>\`, \`<footer>\`, \`<section>\`.\\n- Attributes: \`id\`, \`class\`, \`href\`, \`src\`, \`alt\`.\\n- Forms: \`<form>\`, \`<input>\`, \`<button>\`, \`<select>\`.\\nThe main knowledge of CSS\\n\\n- Selectors: by tag, by class, by ID, nested selectors.\\n- Properties: \`color\`, \`background\`, \`font-size\`, \`margin\`, \`padding\`, \`display\`, \`position\`.\\n- Units: px, em, rem, %, vw, vh.\\n- Responsiveness: media queries (\`@media\`).\\nAn example of use in testing\\nSuppose a button cannot be clicked. The tester looks at the code:\\n\\nThey see the \`disabled\` attribute — that is not a markup bug but the application's logic. Or if an element is hidden:\\n\\nThe tester understands that the element is not displayed because of the CSS rather than because of an error.\\nConclusion\\nKnowing HTML and CSS at a basic level lets a tester find the causes of defects faster, describe them more precisely and work with developers more effectively. It is the mandatory minimum for working with web applications.","<button class=\\"submit-btn\\" disabled>Submit</button>\\n\\n<div style=\\"display: none;\\">A hidden block</div>"],[175,"HTTP responses fall into 5 classes: 1xx (informational) — the request has been accepted and processing continues; 2xx (successful) — the request was processed successfully; 3xx (redirection) — additional actions are required; 4xx (client errors) — the problem is on the client's side; 5xx (server errors) — the problem is on the server's side. Each class has its own range of codes — 200 OK, 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error, for example.","The classes of HTTP response\\nHTTP statuses are grouped into five classes, each of which denotes a particular type of result for the processing of a request. The code's first digit indicates the class: 1xx, 2xx, 3xx, 4xx or 5xx. This is standardized in RFC 7231 and helps developers diagnose problems quickly.\\n1xx — Informational\\nThese codes mean that the request has been accepted and the server is still processing it. They are rarely used in everyday practice but matter for protocols such as WebSocket. An example: \`100 Continue\` — the client may carry on sending the request body.\\n2xx — Successful\\nThe request was processed successfully. The most widespread are: \`200 OK\` (standard success), \`201 Created\` (a resource was created), \`204 No Content\` (success with no response body).\\n3xx — Redirection\\nAdditional actions are required from the client, normally navigating to a different URL. Examples: \`301 Moved Permanently\` (the resource has moved permanently), \`302 Found\` (a temporary redirect), \`304 Not Modified\` (a cached response).\\n4xx — Client errors\\nThe problem is on the client's side: an invalid request, missing permissions or a missing resource. Common ones: \`400 Bad Request\` (incorrect syntax), \`401 Unauthorized\` (authentication is required), \`403 Forbidden\` (access is denied), \`404 Not Found\` (the resource was not found).\\n5xx — Server errors\\nThe server could not fulfil the request because of an internal problem. Examples: \`500 Internal Server Error\` (a general error), \`502 Bad Gateway\` (an invalid response from an upstream server), \`503 Service Unavailable\` (the server is temporarily unavailable).\\nA code example\\n\\nThis example checks the class of the response: 2xx is success, 4xx a client error, 5xx a server error.\\nConclusion\\nKnowing the classes of HTTP status is necessary for handling responses correctly in web applications, debugging APIs and building reliable client-server interactions.","fetch('/api/data')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (response.status === 200) {\\n      return response.json();\\n    } else if (response.status === 404) {\\n      console.error('Resource not found');\\n    } else if (response.status >= 500) {\\n      console.error('Server error');\\n    }\\n  });"],[176,"JSON requires strict syntax: keys and strings in double quotes, numbers without quotes, the boolean values true/false, and null. The data is separated by commas, objects go in curly braces and arrays in square brackets. Trailing commas and comments are not allowed.","The main rules of JSON syntax\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on a subset of JavaScript. Its syntax is strict and requires the following rules to be observed:\\n\\n- Keys and strings are always enclosed in double quotes (\`\\"\`). Single quotes are not allowed.\\n- Numbers are written without quotes and may be integers or floating-point (\`42\`, \`3.14\`, for example).\\n- The boolean values are \`true\` and \`false\` (without quotes).\\n- Null is \`null\` (without quotes).\\n- Objects are enclosed in curly braces \`{}\`, and the key-value pairs are separated by commas.\\n- Arrays are enclosed in square brackets \`[]\`, and the elements are separated by commas.\\n- Trailing commas (a comma after the last element) are forbidden.\\n- Comments are not supported.\\nAn example of valid JSON\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nJSON is used in APIs, configuration files, data storage and exchange between client and server. Observing the syntax is mandatory for parsing and serialization.\\nConclusion: JSON is a simple, strict format that is easy to read and process. Its syntax rules ensure it is unambiguous and compatible between systems.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"isStudent\\": false,\\n  \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", \\"coding\\"],\\n  \\"address\\": null\\n}"],[177,"Errors in JSON can be syntactic: extra commas, the wrong quotes (only double ones are allowed), missing quotes on keys, invalid data types (undefined, for example). Errors can also arise during parsing if the string does not match the format. Problems with escaping characters and with nesting are common.","The main errors in JSON\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for exchanging data, based on JavaScript's syntax. It is stricter, however: keys and strings have to be in double quotes, and values may only be of particular types (string, number, object, array, boolean, null).\\nSyntactic errors\\n\\n- Extra commas: a comma after the last element in an object or array is not allowed. Example: \`{\\"a\\": 1,}\` — an error.\\n- The wrong quotes: only double quotes (\`\\"\`) are allowed. Single quotes or backticks will cause an error.\\n- Missing quotes on keys: keys have to be strings in quotes. \`{key: \\"value\\"}\` is wrong; it has to be \`{\\"key\\": \\"value\\"}\`.\\n- Invalid values: \`undefined\`, \`NaN\` and \`Infinity\` are not supported. Use \`null\`.\\n- Escaping errors: special characters (a quote inside a string, for example) have to be escaped with a backslash: \`\\\\\\"\`.\\nCode examples\\n\\nConclusion\\nKnowing the typical JSON errors helps you avoid problems when passing data between client and server, and when working with configuration files. Always check your JSON with a validator and use try-catch when parsing.","// Invalid JSON\\nconst badJson = '{\\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"age\\": undefined}';\\n// Valid JSON\\nconst goodJson = '{\\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"age\\": null}';\\n\\n// Parsing with error handling\\ntry {\\n  const data = JSON.parse(badJson);\\n} catch (e) {\\n  console.error('Parsing error:', e.message);\\n}"],[178,"JSON supports six data types: string, number, boolean, null, array and object. Strings are always in double quotes, numbers may be integers or floating-point, and arrays and objects can be nested. Other types, such as dates or undefined, are not supported directly.","The main JSON data types\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on a subset of JavaScript's syntax. It supports exactly six data types, which let you represent practically any structured information.\\n\\n- String — a sequence of characters in double quotes; it supports escape sequences (\`\\\\n\`, \`\\\\t\`, for example). Example: \\"Hello, world!\\".\\n- Number — an integer or a floating-point number, which may be negative. It does not support NaN, Infinity or leading zeros. Examples: 42, 3.14, -7.\\n- Boolean — true or false.\\n- Null — a special value denoting the absence of data. It is written as null.\\n- Array — an ordered list of values enclosed in square brackets. The elements may be of any type, including other arrays and objects. Example: [1, \\"two\\", false].\\n- Object — an unordered collection of key-value pairs enclosed in curly braces. The keys are always strings in double quotes. Example: {\\"name\\": \\"Alice\\", \\"age\\": 30}.\\nAn example of use\\n\\nLimitations\\nJSON does not support dates, functions, undefined, symbols or binary data. To pass dates, strings in ISO 8601 format are normally used. There is no support for comments either — that was done to keep parsing simple.\\nConclusion\\nKnowing JSON's data types is necessary for serializing and deserializing data correctly when exchanging it between client and server, and for working with configuration files and APIs.","{\\n  \\"user\\": {\\n    \\"id\\": 123,\\n    \\"name\\": \\"John Doe\\",\\n    \\"active\\": true,\\n    \\"roles\\": [\\"admin\\", \\"editor\\"],\\n    \\"metadata\\": null\\n  }\\n}"],[179,"PSI (PageSpeed Insights) is a tool from Google for analysing the speed and performance of web pages. It provides metrics such as First Contentful Paint (FCP) and Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), along with recommendations for improvement. It is used to uncover bottlenecks in a site's loading and to improve the user experience.","What is PSI?\\nPSI (PageSpeed Insights) is a free tool from Google that analyses the performance of web pages on both mobile devices and desktops. It is based on data from the real user experience (CrUX) and on lab tests (Lighthouse). PSI's main purpose is to help developers understand how quickly their site loads and to give them specific advice on optimization.\\nHow is it used?\\nPSI is used to assess key performance metrics such as First Contentful Paint (FCP), Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) and others. These metrics affect ranking in Google's search results and users' satisfaction. The tool provides numerical scores (from 0 to 100) and detailed reports with recommendations.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you want to check your site's performance. You enter the URL in PSI and get a report. If the LCP exceeds 2.5 seconds, PSI will suggest optimizing the images or using caching. An example of code for optimizing an image:\\n\\nPSI also recommends minifying CSS and JavaScript, using compression and improving the server's response time.\\nConclusion\\nPSI is an indispensable tool for web developers who want to improve loading speed and the user experience. Using it regularly helps keep a site in line with modern performance and SEO standards.","<img src=\\"image.webp\\" loading=\\"lazy\\" alt=\\"A description\\" />"],[180,"A HAR file (HTTP Archive) is a JSON log of all the browser's network requests. It helps developers analyse the loading of resources, server errors and bottlenecks. To analyse one, open the HAR in the developer tools or in a specialized service in order to see the timings, headers and statuses of the requests.","What is a HAR file?\\nHAR (HTTP Archive) is a file format that stores a complete record of all the network interactions between the browser and the server. It contains data about every request: the URL, the method, the headers, the duration, the size of the response and the status. HAR files are indispensable for reproducing and analysing bugs related to performance, the loading of resources or API errors.\\nHow do you create a HAR file?\\nIn Chromium-based browsers (Chrome, Edge), open the developer tools (F12), go to the Network tab, perform the actions that reproduce the bug, then right-click any request and choose 'Save all as HAR with content'. In Firefox it is the same: the Network tab → the 'Export HAR' button.\\nAn example of analysing a HAR\\nSuppose a user complains that a page takes a long time to load. Opening the HAR, you see a request for a large image with a timing of 5 seconds. This indicates that the image needs optimizing or that a CDN should be used. You can also find 404 or 500 errors that are not visible on the page.\\n\\nWhere should you apply it?\\nHAR files are useful when debugging slow loading, API errors, caching problems and mismatched headers. They let developers and testers reproduce the environment exactly and find the root of the problem.\\nConclusion: HAR files are a powerful tool for analysing network bugs and optimizing performance, especially when working with client-server applications.","// An example of a HAR's structure (simplified)\\n{\\n  \\"log\\": {\\n    \\"entries\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\"request\\": {\\n          \\"url\\": \\"https://example.com/api/data\\",\\n          \\"method\\": \\"GET\\"\\n        },\\n        \\"response\\": {\\n          \\"status\\": 200,\\n          \\"content\\": {\\n            \\"size\\": 1234\\n          }\\n        },\\n        \\"timings\\": {\\n          \\"wait\\": 300,\\n          \\"receive\\": 50\\n        }\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}"],[181,"Testing filters involves checking that each filter works correctly on its own, that their combinations work, and that boundary values are handled. It is important to check that the filters display the filtered data correctly and do not break other parts of the interface. It is also worth testing performance with a large amount of data.","The main aspects of testing filters\\nTesting the filters on a site is the process of checking that the mechanisms for selecting data by given criteria work correctly. It is necessary in order to ensure that the user's search for information is accurate and convenient. The main kinds of test include functional testing, boundary-value testing and integration testing.\\nFunctional testing\\nCheck that every filter works in isolation. If there is a price filter, for example, make sure that when the range from 100 to 200 is selected, only the products in that price segment are displayed.\\n\\nTesting combinations of filters\\nCheck that several filters work together correctly. A filter by category and one by price, for example, should intersect rather than replace one another.\\nBoundary values\\nTest the extreme cases: empty results, the maximum and minimum values, and invalid input (negative numbers, for example).\\nIntegration testing\\nMake sure the filters do not break other components such as pagination or sorting. After a filter has been applied, for example, the pagination should recalculate the number of pages.\\nConclusion: testing filters is necessary in order to ensure the user interface is reliable and convenient. It matters especially in online shops, catalogues and any system with a large volume of data, where the accuracy of filtering directly affects the user experience.","// An example of checking a price filter\\nconst items = [\\n  { name: 'A', price: 150 },\\n  { name: 'B', price: 250 },\\n  { name: 'C', price: 50 }\\n];\\nconst filterByPrice = (items, min, max) =>\\n  items.filter(item => item.price >= min && item.price <= max);\\nconsole.log(filterByPrice(items, 100, 200)); // [{ name: 'A', price: 150 }]"],[182,"The Network tab in DevTools is used to monitor all the network requests a page makes: loading the HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images, and API requests. It helps you analyse loading times, response statuses and file sizes and uncover bottlenecks. It is a key tool for optimizing performance and debugging network problems.","The purpose of the Network tab\\nThe Network tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) is intended for tracking and analysing all the network requests a web page performs. It lets a developer see which resources are loading, how long it takes and which errors arise. It is an indispensable tool for optimizing performance, debugging API integrations and understanding how an application behaves on the network.\\nThe main capabilities\\n\\n- Viewing the requests: it displays every request (XHR, Fetch, images, scripts, styles) in chronological order.\\n- The details of a request: clicking a request lets you see its Headers, Response body, Payload, Timing and cookies.\\n- Filtering: you can filter the requests by type (XHR, JS, CSS, Img, Media, Font, Doc, WS) or by text in the URL.\\n- Analysing performance: the Timing tab shows how long the DNS resolution, the connection, the TLS handshake, the sending of the request and the receiving of the response took.\\n- Emulating conditions: you can simulate a slow connection (throttling) or disable the cache.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you are developing an application in React that loads data through an API. You want to check that the request is sent correctly and that the response arrives without errors. Open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab and reload the page or perform the action that triggers the request. You will see the request to your API. Click it:\\n\\nIn Network you will see the response status (200 OK, for example), the headers (Content-Type, Authorization), the response body (JSON with the data) and the duration. If the status is 404 or 500, you will notice it at once and be able to fix it.\\nConclusion\\nThe Network tab is the main tool for debugging the network interaction of web applications. It helps you find problems with the loading of resources quickly, analyse performance and check that API requests are correct. Use it during development and testing in order to ensure your application works reliably.","// An example of code that makes a request\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[183,"Qt is a cross-platform framework for developing applications in C++. It is used to create graphical interfaces, desktop and mobile applications, and embedded systems. Qt provides a rich set of libraries for working with networking, databases, multimedia and graphics. Thanks to its modularity and its support for many platforms, Qt is popular in the industry for building complex interfaces.","Qt is a powerful cross-platform framework written in C++ that lets you develop applications with a graphical interface (GUI) for various operating systems, including Windows, macOS, Linux, Android and iOS. It was created by the Qt Group and is widely used in industry, from desktop programs to embedded systems.\\nQt's main capabilities\\n\\n- Cross-platform support: the same code can be compiled for different operating systems without changes.\\n- A rich set of widgets: buttons, tables, trees, text fields and other interface elements.\\n- Signals and slots: a mechanism for connecting events and handlers that simplifies development.\\n- Modularity: Qt consists of many modules, such as QtWidgets, QtNetwork, QtSQL, QtMultimedia and others.\\nAn example of a simple application in Qt\\n\\nThis code creates a window with a button. Qt handles the events and the drawing automatically.\\nWhere is Qt applied?\\n\\n- Desktop applications: VLC Media Player, VirtualBox and Telegram Desktop, for example.\\n- Embedded systems: Qt for MCU lets you run interfaces on microcontrollers.\\n- Mobile applications: Qt supports Android and iOS.\\n- Industry: control systems, medical equipment, automotive interfaces.\\nConclusion: Qt is ideally suited to building cross-platform applications with a rich interface, especially when high performance and support for many platforms are required. It is worth choosing for projects where reliability and long-term support matter.","#include <QApplication>\\n#include <QPushButton>\\n\\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\\n    QApplication app(argc, argv);\\n    QPushButton button(\\"Hello, Qt!\\");\\n    button.resize(200, 100);\\n    button.show();\\n    return app.exec();\\n}"],[184,"Whether an implementation matches the design mockups is checked by visual comparison (pixel perfect) with tools such as Figma, Percy or Chromatic. Automated visual regression tests are also used. It is important to check not only the dimensions and colours but also the spacing, the typography and the behaviour at different resolutions.","The process of checking against the design mockups\\nChecking that an implementation matches the design mockups is a key stage of quality control for markup. It involves both manual and automated methods, in order to be sure that the interface looks and behaves the way the designer intended.\\nManual checking (pixel perfect)\\nThe developer or QA engineer overlays a screenshot of the finished page on the mockup in Figma or Photoshop, using an overlay mode. The following are checked:\\n\\n- The dimensions and spacing of elements\\n- Colours and gradients\\n- Typography (fonts, sizes, line heights)\\n- The states of elements (hover, active, focus)\\nAutomated checking (visual regression testing)\\nTools such as Percy, Chromatic or Applitools take screenshots of components and compare them with reference images. When a difference is found, a notification is sent. An example of the configuration for Storybook with Chromatic:\\n\\nTools for checking\\n\\n- Figma — manual comparison through overlaying\\n- Percy / Chromatic — automated visual regression testing\\n- BrowserStack — checking on real devices\\nConclusion: checking against the mockups regularly helps you avoid an accumulation of visual defects and speeds up sign-off with the designer. Automation is especially useful in large projects with frequent changes.","// .storybook/main.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  stories: ['../src/**/*.stories.@(js|jsx|ts|tsx)'],\\n  addons: ['@storybook/addon-essentials'],\\n  framework: '@storybook/react',\\n};\\n// Running it: npx chromatic --project-token=YOUR_TOKEN"],[185,"To debug a frontend application, the browser's built-in developer tools (DevTools) are used. The main capabilities are: inspecting and changing the HTML/CSS, setting breakpoints in JavaScript code, analysing network requests and profiling performance. DevTools can be opened with the F12 key or through the context menu.","The basics of debugging in the browser\\nThe developer tools (DevTools) are a set of facilities built into the browser that let you analyse and debug frontend applications. They are available in every modern browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari) and are opened with the F12 key or through the context menu.\\nThe key capabilities of DevTools\\n\\n- Elements (the inspector) — inspecting and editing the HTML structure and CSS styles in real time. It lets you experiment with your markup without reloading the page.\\n- Console — the output of errors, warnings and logs. You can run arbitrary JavaScript code in order to test a hypothesis.\\n- Sources — inspecting the source code, setting breakpoints, stepping through the code and viewing the values of variables.\\n- Network — analysing every network request: loading time, headers, response body. It helps you diagnose problems with an API or with the loading of resources.\\n- Performance — recording and analysing a page's performance: loading, rendering, the execution of scripts.\\nAn example of using breakpoints\\n\\nWorking with source maps\\nIf the code is minified or transpiled (from TypeScript or React, for example), source maps let you see the original source code rather than the compiled output. To get this, the generation of source maps has to be enabled in your bundler (Webpack, Vite).\\nConclusion\\nDevTools are an indispensable tool for every frontend developer. They let you find and fix errors in markup, logic and performance quickly without leaving the browser.","// The source code of the function that needs debugging\\nfunction calculateTotal(price, tax) {\\n  const taxAmount = price * tax;\\n  const total = price + taxAmount;\\n  return total;\\n}\\n\\n// In the DevTools Sources tab we find this file,\\n// and click the line number of const total = ... in order to set a breakpoint.\\n// When the function is called, execution will stop on that line,\\n// and you can look at the values of price, tax and taxAmount in the Scope panel."],[186,"A slow-loading site can be diagnosed through the Network tab in the browser's developer tools. There you can see the loading time of every resource, the sizes of the files and the order in which they load. You can also use Lighthouse to audit performance and get recommendations. Analysing the waterfall chart helps you uncover blocking requests or images that are too large.","Diagnosing a slow-loading site without access to the code\\nTo diagnose a site's performance without access to the source code you can use the browser's built-in tools. The main one is the Network tab in Chrome DevTools, which shows every network request, its size and its duration.\\nThe main diagnostic steps\\n\\n- Open DevTools (F12) and go to the Network tab.\\n- Reload the page and study the waterfall chart — it shows the sequence in which the resources load.\\n- Pay attention to long requests (red or orange bars) — they indicate slow resources.\\n- Check the sizes of the images, scripts and styles — large files can slow loading down.\\n- Use Lighthouse (the Audits tab) for an automatic performance audit.\\nAn example of the analysis\\nSuppose you see that loading the home page takes 5 seconds. In the waterfall you can see that a large JavaScript file (2 MB) loads at the start and blocks the rendering. This indicates that the script needs optimizing or should be loaded asynchronously.\\n\\nConclusion\\nDiagnosing through the browser's tools lets you uncover the main performance problems quickly without access to the server. This is useful to frontend developers and QA engineers when optimizing the user experience.","// An example: analysing loading times through the Performance API (where available)\\nperformance.getEntriesByType('resource').forEach(entry => {\\n  console.log(entry.name, entry.duration);\\n});"],[187,"In the Network tab you can see every request a page makes: HTML, CSS, JS, images, API calls. It is useful to look at the response status (200, 404, 500), the loading time, the file size and the request and response headers. This helps you debug errors, optimize loading speed and check that the data being sent is correct.","The main parts of the Network tab\\nThe Network tab in the browser's developer tools shows every network request a web page performs. It is a key tool for debugging and optimizing performance.\\nWhat is worth analysing\\n\\n- The status — the HTTP code: 200 (success), 404 (not found), 500 (a server error), for example. It lets you uncover problems with the loading of resources quickly.\\n- The type — document, script, stylesheet, xhr/fetch, image and so on. It helps you understand which resources are loading.\\n- The time (Time / Waterfall) — it shows how long each stage took: DNS, the connection, sending, waiting, receiving. Useful for finding bottlenecks.\\n- The size — the size of the data transferred (compressed and uncompressed). Large files can slow loading down.\\n- The headers — information about the request and the response: Content-Type, Cache-Control, Authorization and others. Important for debugging APIs and caching.\\n- Payload / Preview / Response — the body of the request (for POST) and of the response. It lets you check which data is being sent and received.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose a page is not loading an image. We open Network and find a request of type image with status 404. We look at the URL — the path may be wrong. Or we see that a request to the API returns 500 — which means the problem is on the server.\\n\\nIn Network you can see this request, its status, its headers and its response body.\\nConclusion\\nThe Network tab is an indispensable tool for debugging network interaction, checking that an API is correct, analysing loading performance and uncovering errors on the client's or the server's side.","// An example: checking an API response through fetch\\nfetch('/api/users')\\n  .then(response => {\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      console.error('Error:', response.status);\\n    }\\n    return response.json();\\n  })\\n  .then(data => console.log(data));"],[188,"A design system is a set of standards, rules and ready-made components for building consistent interfaces. It includes UI components, design tokens (colours, fonts), guidelines and documentation. Using a design system speeds development up, makes maintenance easier and ensures products are consistent. Examples: Material Design, Ant Design.","What is a design system?\\nA design system is a comprehensive set of standards, principles, rules and ready-made components that ensures user interfaces are consistent across all of a company's products. It is not merely a library of UI components but a coherent system including design tokens (colours, typography, spacing), interaction patterns, accessibility guidelines and documentation.\\nThe main components\\n\\n- UI components: buttons, inputs, modal windows, tables and so on.\\n- Design tokens: variables for colours, fonts and spacing (\`--color-primary: #1890ff\`, for example).\\n- Patterns: recurring solutions for typical tasks (navigation, forms, filters).\\n- Documentation: the rules of use, code examples, best practices.\\nAn example implementation in React\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nA design system is used in large projects with several teams of developers and designers. It is critically important for products that require a single user experience (corporate portals, SaaS platforms, mobile applications, for example).\\nConclusion: a design system is worth introducing when a company has several products or teams, in order to avoid fragmented interfaces, speed development up and make the code easier to maintain.","// An example of using design tokens in CSS-in-JS\\nconst theme = {\\n  colors: {\\n    primary: '#1890ff',\\n    background: '#f0f2f5',\\n  },\\n  spacing: (n) => \`\${n * 8}px\`,\\n};\\n\\n// A button component from the design system\\nconst Button = styled.button\`\\n  background: \${props => props.theme.colors.primary};\\n  padding: \${props => props.theme.spacing(2)} \${props => props.theme.spacing(4)};\\n  border: none;\\n  border-radius: 4px;\\n  color: white;\\n  cursor: pointer;\\n\`;"],[189,"The main cookie attributes are: Name, Value, Domain, Path, Expires/Max-Age, Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite. Name and Value set the name and the value. Domain and Path determine which URLs the cookie is sent for. Expires or Max-Age set its lifetime. Secure requires HTTPS. HttpOnly forbids access through JavaScript. SameSite restricts sending it in cross-site requests.","The main cookie attributes\\nCookies are small fragments of data that the server sends to the browser; the browser stores them and sends them back with every request to the server. A cookie's attributes control its behaviour, its security and its lifetime.\\nThe list of attributes\\n\\n- Name — the cookie's name (required).\\n- Value — the cookie's value (required).\\n- Domain — the domain the cookie is valid for (example.com, for example).\\n- Path — the URL path the cookie is sent for (/, for example).\\n- Expires — the expiry date (in HTTP-date format).\\n- Max-Age — the lifetime in seconds (it takes priority over Expires).\\n- Secure — a flag indicating that the cookie must be transmitted only over HTTPS.\\n- HttpOnly — a flag forbidding access to the cookie through JavaScript (document.cookie).\\n- SameSite — restricts the sending of the cookie in cross-site requests (Strict, Lax, None).\\nAn example of setting a cookie on the server (Node.js with Express)\\n\\nConclusion\\nA cookie's attributes let you configure its behaviour precisely, from restricting its scope to increasing its security. Use Secure and HttpOnly to protect against interception and XSS attacks, and SameSite to prevent CSRF attacks.","res.cookie('sessionId', 'abc123', {\\n  domain: '.example.com',\\n  path: '/',\\n  maxAge: 3600000, // 1 hour\\n  secure: true,\\n  httpOnly: true,\\n  sameSite: 'strict'\\n});"],[190,"Selectors by class and by ID are considered the most stable, since they are less exposed to changes in the HTML structure. Selectors by tag and by attribute are less stable, while pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements can change depending on state. Using classes with meaningful names increases the stability and readability of your code.","What is the stability of a selector?\\nThe stability of a CSS selector means how likely it is that the style applied with it will not break when the HTML structure or the content changes. Stable selectors help you avoid unexpected changes to your markup and make the code easier to maintain.\\nWhich selectors are considered the most stable?\\n\\n- Selectors by class (.class) — the most stable, since classes are normally assigned for a particular purpose and rarely change. They have medium specificity and are easy to override.\\n- Selectors by ID (#id) — very stable, but they have high specificity, which can make overriding styles harder. They are used for unique elements.\\n- Selectors by attribute ([attr]) — stable, provided the attributes do not change dynamically. They suit styling elements with particular properties.\\n- Selectors by tag (element) — less stable, since tags can be reused in different contexts. Changing them may affect a great many elements.\\n- Pseudo-classes (:hover, :focus) — they depend on an element's state and can change, so they are less stable for permanent styles.\\nCode examples\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo create stable, easily maintainable styles it is recommended to use selectors by class with meaningful names. This reduces the risk of breaking your markup when things change and makes working as a team easier.","/* A stable selector by class */\\n.button {\\n  background-color: blue;\\n  color: white;\\n}\\n\\n/* A less stable selector by tag */\\nbutton {\\n  border: none;\\n  padding: 10px;\\n}\\n\\n/* A selector by ID with high specificity */\\n#submit-btn {\\n  font-size: 16px;\\n}"],[191,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on the syntax of JavaScript objects. It uses \\"key-value\\" pairs and supports strings, numbers, arrays, objects, booleans and null. JSON is widely applied to pass data between server and client in web applications.","What is JSON?\\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for storing and transmitting structured data. It is based on the syntax of JavaScript objects but is language-independent, which makes it universal for exchanging data between various systems.\\nJSON's syntax\\nJSON supports the following data types:\\n\\n- Strings (in double quotes)\\n- Numbers (integers and floating-point)\\n- Booleans (true/false)\\n- null\\n- Arrays (ordered lists in square brackets)\\n- Objects (unordered collections of \\"key-value\\" pairs in curly braces)\\nAn example of a JSON object:\\n\\nThe uses of JSON\\nJSON is used in APIs to pass data between client and server, in configuration files, in databases (MongoDB, for example) and for serializing objects. It has replaced XML in many web applications thanks to its simplicity and readability.\\nAn example of working with JSON in JavaScript\\n\\nConclusion: JSON is the standard format for exchanging data; it is simple to use, human-readable and supported by practically every programming language. It is worth applying wherever structured data has to be passed, especially in web development and microservice architectures.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30,\\n  \\"isStudent\\": false,\\n  \\"hobbies\\": [\\"reading\\", \\"coding\\"],\\n  \\"address\\": {\\n    \\"city\\": \\"New York\\",\\n    \\"zip\\": \\"10001\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Converting an object into a JSON string\\nconst user = { name: \\"Bob\\", age: 25 };\\nconst jsonString = JSON.stringify(user);\\nconsole.log(jsonString); // {\\"name\\":\\"Bob\\",\\"age\\":25}\\n\\n// Parsing a JSON string into an object\\nconst parsedUser = JSON.parse(jsonString);\\nconsole.log(parsedUser.name); // Bob"],[192,"Testing a payment form involves checking the validation of the card number, the expiry date, the CVV and the cardholder's name. You have to check the handling of errors on incorrect data, support for different card types and the security of the data transfer. It is also important to test successful and unsuccessful payment scenarios through the payment gateway.","The main aspects of testing a payment form\\nTesting a bank card payment form requires attention to input validation, error handling and security. It is important to check that the form accepts the data correctly, displays errors and interacts with the payment gateway.\\nValidating the fields\\n\\n- The card number: checking the length (16 digits for Visa/Mastercard), the format (groups of 4 digits) and the Luhn algorithm.\\n- The expiry date: checking the MM/YY format and that the date has not passed.\\n- The CVV: 3-4 digits, digits only.\\n- The cardholder's name: letters, spaces and hyphens only.\\nAn example test in JavaScript\\n\\nError handling\\nCheck that the form shows clear error messages for every field — \\"Invalid card number\\" or \\"The card has expired\\", for example. It is also important to test scenarios with empty fields and with incorrect formats.\\nSecurity\\nMake sure the data is transmitted over HTTPS, is not saved in logs and is not displayed in the console. Use tokenization or PCI DSS compliant solutions.\\nConclusion\\nTesting a payment form is critical for security and UX. Apply automated tests for validation and manual ones for checking scenarios with the payment gateway.","function validateCardNumber(number) {\\n  const cleaned = number.replace(/\\\\s/g, '');\\n  if (!/^\\\\d{16}$/.test(cleaned)) return false;\\n  let sum = 0;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < cleaned.length; i++) {\\n    let digit = parseInt(cleaned[i]);\\n    if (i % 2 === 0) {\\n      digit *= 2;\\n      if (digit > 9) digit -= 9;\\n    }\\n    sum += digit;\\n  }\\n  return sum % 10 === 0;\\n}"],[193,"To improve the UX of a payment form it is worth adding popular payment methods such as credit cards, PayPal, Apple Pay, Google Pay and local methods. It is important to minimize the number of fields, to use autofill and to show security indicators. This reduces friction and increases conversion.","Improving the UX of a payment form\\nThe payment form is a critical stage of the sales funnel. Poor UX can lead to abandoned carts. The main goal is to make the process fast, safe and intuitive.\\nThe choice of payment methods\\nAdding a variety of payment methods makes things more convenient for users. It is recommended to include:\\n\\n- Credit and debit cards (Visa, Mastercard, Mir)\\n- Electronic wallets (PayPal, WebMoney, Qiwi)\\n- Mobile payments (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay)\\n- Local methods (SBP, Alipay, iDEAL — depending on the region)\\nA code example: a simple payment form with validation\\n\\nAdditional UX improvements\\n\\n- Autofill for the fields (autocomplete=\\"cc-number\\")\\n- Real-time validation with errors highlighted\\n- Security indicators (a padlock icon, an SSL certificate)\\n- The ability to save a card for future purchases\\n- Minimizing the number of steps (single-page checkout)\\nConclusion: improving the UX of a payment form directly affects conversion and customer satisfaction. It is recommended to test different variants and analyse users' behaviour.","<form id=\\"payment-form\\">\\n  <label for=\\"card-number\\">Card number</label>\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" id=\\"card-number\\" placeholder=\\"1234 5678 9012 3456\\" required>\\n  <label for=\\"expiry\\">Expiry date</label>\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" id=\\"expiry\\" placeholder=\\"MM/YY\\" required>\\n  <label for=\\"cvv\\">CVV</label>\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" id=\\"cvv\\" placeholder=\\"123\\" required>\\n  <button type=\\"submit\\">Pay</button>\\n</form>"],[194,"In the Network panel find the request you want. Right-click it and choose \\"Replay XHR\\" or \\"Edit and Replay\\". This lets you test an API quickly without reloading the page.","Resending a request through DevTools\\nThe browser's developer tools (DevTools) provide a convenient way of resending an HTTP request without having to reload the page or write code. This is especially useful when debugging an API, testing changes on the server or checking the responses to different parameters.\\nHow to do it\\n\\n- Open DevTools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I).\\n- Go to the Network tab.\\n- Find the request you want in the list (you can filter by type — XHR, for example).\\n- Right-click the request.\\n- Choose Replay XHR — the request will be sent with the same headers and body.\\n- To change the parameters, choose Edit and Replay — an editor opens where you can change the method, the URL, the headers or the request body.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you are testing a POST request that creates a user. After the first response you want to check how the server reacts to the same data being sent again. Instead of writing curl or a script, simply use Replay XHR in DevTools.\\n\\nAfter it has been sent you will see the request in Network. Right-click -> Replay XHR will send it again.\\nConclusion\\nThe ability to resend requests in DevTools speeds up the debugging and testing of APIs, letting you check changes quickly without additional tools. It is an indispensable skill for frontend and backend developers.","// An example of a request that can be replayed\\nfetch('/api/users', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'John' })\\n});"],[195,"The Fetch/XHR tab in DevTools shows every network request the browser has sent. It lets you inspect the headers, the request and response bodies, the status codes and the durations. This helps you debug API errors, check the data being passed and analyse performance.","The purpose of the Fetch/XHR tab\\nThe Fetch/XHR tab in the browser's developer tools (DevTools) is intended for monitoring and debugging the asynchronous HTTP requests performed through the Fetch API or XMLHttpRequest (XHR). It is part of the Network panel and filters out everything except the requests initiated by JavaScript code, rather than by the loading of the page or its resources.\\nHow it works\\nWhen a web application sends a request to the server (to get data or submit a form, for example), the browser records it in its logs. The Fetch/XHR tab displays a list of such requests with the key information:\\n\\n- The request's URL\\n- The method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE and so on)\\n- The response's status code (200, 404, 500 and others)\\n- The type and size of the response\\n- The duration\\nClicking a particular request opens the details: the headers, the request and response bodies, the cookies and a timeline.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you are developing an application in React that loads a list of users through the Fetch API. If the data is not displayed, open DevTools (F12), go to the Network tab and select the Fetch/XHR filter. You will see the request to the API. Check the status code: if it is 200, the server replied successfully. Then look at the response body in the Response tab — the structure of the data may not match what you expect. If the status is 404, check that the URL is correct.\\n\\nIn DevTools you will see this request, its headers and its response, which will help you find the problem quickly.\\nConclusion\\nThe Fetch/XHR tab is an indispensable tool for debugging client-server interaction. It lets you uncover errors in requests quickly, check that the data is correct and optimize the performance of network operations.","// An example of code that may cause an error\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));"],[196,"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data-interchange format based on the syntax of JavaScript objects. It is used to pass structured data between server and client in web applications, and also to store configuration. JSON is easy for both humans and machines to read, which makes it popular in REST APIs, in databases (MongoDB, for example) and in configuration files.","JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for exchanging data, based on the syntax of JavaScript objects. It was developed to represent simple data structures and associative arrays (objects). JSON is language-independent, which lets it be used in practically any programming environment.\\nThe main areas where JSON is applied\\n\\n- Web APIs and REST services: JSON is the standard for passing data between a client (a browser, for example) and a server. Most modern APIs return their data in JSON format.\\n- Configuration files: many tools and frameworks (Webpack, ESLint, npm, for example) use JSON to store a project's settings.\\n- Storing data: NoSQL databases such as MongoDB store documents in a format close to JSON (BSON).\\n- Exchanging data between services: in a microservice architecture JSON is often used to serialize the messages passed between components.\\nAn example of using JSON in JavaScript\\n\\nAn example of using JSON in Python\\n\\nJSON supports only a few data types: strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays and objects. This makes it simple but sufficient for most data-exchange tasks.\\nConclusion: JSON is applied wherever a lightweight, readable, cross-platform format for passing or storing structured data is required. It is especially useful in web development, configuration and microservices, replacing more cumbersome formats such as XML.","// A JavaScript object\\nconst user = {\\n  name: \\"Alice\\",\\n  age: 30,\\n  isAdmin: false\\n};\\n\\n// Serializing it into a JSON string\\nconst jsonString = JSON.stringify(user);\\nconsole.log(jsonString); // {\\"name\\":\\"Alice\\",\\"age\\":30,\\"isAdmin\\":false}\\n\\n// Parsing the JSON string back into an object\\nconst parsedUser = JSON.parse(jsonString);\\nconsole.log(parsedUser.name); // Alice\\n\\nimport json\\n\\n# A Python dictionary\\ndata = {\\n    \\"name\\": \\"Bob\\",\\n    \\"age\\": 25,\\n    \\"skills\\": [\\"Python\\", \\"JSON\\"]\\n}\\n\\n# Serializing it into JSON\\njson_data = json.dumps(data)\\nprint(json_data)  # {\\"name\\": \\"Bob\\", \\"age\\": 25, \\"skills\\": [\\"Python\\", \\"JSON\\"]}\\n\\n# Parsing the JSON\\nparsed_data = json.loads(json_data)\\nprint(parsed_data[\\"name\\"])  # Bob"],[197,"For text fields you have to check the length and the format (an email address or a phone number, for example), the absence of malicious characters (XSS, SQL injection), and sanitize the input. It is important to check the data both on the client and on the server. Use whitelists of permitted characters and avoid blacklists.","The main input checks for text fields\\nChecking input is a critically important stage of handling data that prevents a great many vulnerabilities, such as XSS, SQL injection and data corruption. It has to be performed both on the client side (for the user's convenience) and on the server side (for security).\\nThe kinds of check\\n\\n- Checking the length: limiting the minimum and maximum number of characters in order to avoid buffer overflows or excessively long strings.\\n- Checking the format: using regular expressions to check email addresses, phone numbers, URLs and other structured data.\\n- Sanitization: removing or escaping dangerous characters such as \`<script>\` or \`' OR 1=1 --\`.\\n- A whitelist of characters: permitting only particular characters (letters and digits only, for example) rather than trying to block every dangerous one.\\nA code example in JavaScript (a server-side check)\\n\\nConclusion\\nChecking input is mandatory for every text field, especially in web applications that work with user data. It protects against attacks and ensures the integrity of the data. Always use server-side validation as the main barrier, and client-side validation as an additional convenience.","function validateInput(input) {\\n  // Checking the length\\n  if (input.length < 1 || input.length > 100) {\\n    return 'The length must be between 1 and 100 characters';\\n  }\\n  // Checking for dangerous characters (XSS)\\n  const dangerous = /[<>\\"'\\\\\\\\/]/g;\\n  if (dangerous.test(input)) {\\n    return 'Invalid characters';\\n  }\\n  // Escaping for safe output\\n  const sanitized = input.replace(/&/g, '&amp;')\\n                         .replace(/</g, '&lt;')\\n                         .replace(/>/g, '&gt;');\\n  return sanitized;\\n}"],[198,"The checksum of a bank card number is its last digit, computed with the Luhn algorithm. It is used to check that a card number is valid without contacting the bank. The algorithm works on the digits of the number: it doubles every second digit, sums all the digits, and if the sum is divisible by 10 the number is considered valid. This helps catch typos during entry.","What is the checksum of a bank card number?\\nThe checksum of a bank card number is the number's last digit, which is computed with the Luhn algorithm. This algorithm is used to check that a card number is valid without any need to contact the payment system or the bank. It helps catch accidental errors during entry, such as transposed digits or typos.\\nHow does the Luhn algorithm work?\\nThe Luhn algorithm is applied to the card number (normally 16 digits). The last digit is the check digit. The verification process involves the following steps:\\n\\n- Starting from the first digit on the left, double every second digit.\\n- If the result of the doubling is greater than 9, subtract 9 (or add the digits of the number together).\\n- Add up all the digits (including the ones that were not doubled).\\n- If the total is divisible by 10 with no remainder, the number is valid.\\nAn example for the number 4539 1488 0343 6467 (the checksum is 7):\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nThe Luhn algorithm is used in payment systems (Visa, MasterCard) and also in other identifiers such as IMEIs and social security numbers. It is built into libraries for validating forms on sites and in mobile applications.\\nConclusion: a checksum based on the Luhn algorithm is a simple, effective way of guarding against accidental entry errors. It is worth applying wherever card numbers or other identifiers have to be typed in by hand, in order to reduce the number of invalid transactions.","// We double every second digit (starting from the first):\\n4*2=8, 3*2=6, 1*2=2, 8*2=16->1+6=7, 0*2=0, 4*2=8, 6*2=12->1+2=3, 6*2=12->1+2=3\\n// We add up all the digits: 8+5+6+9+2+4+7+8+0+3+8+3+3+4+3+7 = 80\\n// 80 is divisible by 10 -> the number is valid"],[199,"The main browser tools for diagnosis are the Elements, Console, Sources, Network, Performance and Application tabs. Elements lets you inspect and edit the HTML/CSS. Console prints JavaScript errors and logs. Sources is used to debug code with breakpoints. Network shows every network request and its duration. Performance analyses a page's performance. Application manages storage, cookies and the cache.","Browser tools for diagnosing frontend problems\\nModern browsers provide a powerful set of built-in developer tools (DevTools) that help you uncover and fix problems in web applications. The main tabs include Elements, Console, Sources, Network, Performance and Application. Each of them solves specific diagnostic tasks.\\nThe main tabs and their uses\\n\\n- Elements — inspecting and editing the DOM tree and the CSS styles in real time. It lets you check quickly how changes affect the appearance.\\n- Console — printing JavaScript errors, warnings and logs. You can run arbitrary code for testing.\\n- Sources — debugging JavaScript with breakpoints, stepping through, inspecting the call stack and the values of variables.\\n- Network — monitoring every HTTP request, its status, duration, headers and body. It helps you find slow or failed requests.\\n- Performance — recording and analysing a page's performance: loading, rendering, the execution of scripts. It lets you uncover bottlenecks.\\n- Application — managing local storage, sessions, cookies, IndexedDB, the Service Worker cache and PWA manifests.\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose a page is loading slowly. Open the Network tab and reload the page. You will see every request and its duration. If some request takes a long time, check its size or the server's latency. Then go to Performance, press Record and repeat the action. The analysis will show which functions or which rendering take the most time.\\n\\nYou can also use breakpoints in Sources. If a function does not return the expected result, for example, set a breakpoint on its call and check the arguments.\\nConclusion\\nThe browser's tools are indispensable for quickly diagnosing and debugging frontend work. They let you find errors in your code, optimize performance and analyse network interaction without any additional libraries.","// An example of code for debugging in the Console\\nconsole.log('A debugging message');\\nconsole.error('Error: something went wrong');\\nconsole.table([{name: 'Alice', age: 30}, {name: 'Bob', age: 25}]);"],[200,"HTTP-only is a flag that forbids access to a cookie from JavaScript (document.cookie). Cookies with this flag are sent only with HTTP requests to the server. This protects session tokens from being stolen through XSS attacks. The flag is set by the server in the Set-Cookie header.","What is an HTTP-only cookie?\\nHTTP-only is a security attribute that can be added to an HTTP cookie. When a cookie is marked HTTP-only, the browser forbids access to it from client-side JavaScript (through \`document.cookie\`). Such cookies are sent by the browser automatically only with HTTP requests to the server that set them.\\nWhy is this needed?\\nThe main purpose is protection against cross-site scripting (XSS). If an attacker injects a malicious script into a page, they will not be able to read HTTP-only cookies — a session token, for example. This reduces the risk of session hijacking considerably.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe server sets the cookie with the \`HttpOnly\` flag in its response header:\\n\\nAfter that the browser stores the cookie, but JavaScript cannot read it:\\n\\nThe cookie is still sent with every request to the server automatically.\\nWhere is it applied?\\nHTTP-only cookies are widely used to store session identifiers, authentication tokens and other confidential data that should not be available to client-side code. It is standard practice in web development for increasing security.\\nConclusion\\nUse the HTTP-only flag for every cookie containing sensitive data, especially session tokens. It is a mandatory measure of protection against XSS attacks, but it is not a panacea — combine it with other security mechanisms such as Secure, SameSite and CSP.","Set-Cookie: sessionId=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict\\n\\n// An attempt to read the cookie from JS\\nconsole.log(document.cookie); // Will print an empty string, or the other cookies without HttpOnly"]]`);export{e as default};